":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe","id":44068562,"login":"Leikoe","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0MDY4NTYy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/4a642f36a7c543daa9d956bbd915f0000d499d0a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-01T23:15:57Z","email":"44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Léo"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-01T23:15:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"shapes and indexing: the design, its contract, and the first two stagings (#24)\n\n* shapes and indexing: the design, and its contract in the type system\n\nThe Γ treatment applied to movement, with the tinygrad indexing/layout\npath read end to end as the foil (written up in vs_tinygrad.md):\n\n- shapes_and_indexing.md: a movement node denotes a linear correspondence\n  stored in its one affine direction (Reindex = read, View = write);\n  every structural question — six mechanisms today, two of them stating\n  contradictory laws — is a query against it. Finish ir::Resolver into\n  the one answerer; no second mechanism. Staging carries a driver per\n  step; nothing ships without one.\n\n- tests/shapes_contract.rs: the model before the implementation, as\n  gamma.rs began. Law 1 is mint privacy, Law 3 is the field list (no\n  div, no mod, no data term), Law 5 is cap() total / at(bindings)\n  partial, and the adjoint classification is a theorem: symbolic verdict\n  from coefficients equals enumerated preimage structure over the whole\n  zoo. merge.transposed() == split is a passing test, not a claim.\n\n- vs_tinygrad.md: line accounting on their own sz.py metric (sanic core\n  is 41% of theirs by token), the indexing/layout path end to end, and\n  design-debt 4 marked paid (df77288 killed the 0·Iota order trick).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* examples: delete the attic\n\n4,428 lines Cargo never compiled (no auto-discovery under a\nsubdirectory), which means never verified, which means already rotten.\nGit history keeps every line; the two living examples stay.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* shapes: the chain-depth bet, measured\n\nProbe on the real llama decode graph at ctx 1030: 2,070 nodes, 760\nmovement, maximal View/Reindex chain depth 4. Factored-on-demand walks\nstay cheap; the composed-map cache stays unbuilt.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* metal: one polarity loop where the sched emitter had six mirrors\n\nEvery sliced/unsliced pair in emit_fused_metal_sched_with iterated the\nslot partition twice with the branch inverted, differing only in\npolarity and a name prefix — the accessor closures already chose the\nper-slot names. slots_where iterates one polarity; the into/combine\npairs render through one loop over (exprs, name); the threadgroup\ncombine renders once for both polarities.\n\nNo behavior: the generated MSL for the llama decode program is\nbyte-identical, and that program exercises both polarities (the flash\nfold lane-slices its o slot against unsliced m/ℓ, including the\nmixed-accessor merge). 256 tests green across all correctness targets;\nthe measurement probes were skipped — they time GPU behavior a\nbyte-identical emitter cannot change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* metal: the two fold emitters share their frame, window, and store\n\nemit_fused_metal_with and emit_fused_metal_sched_with each carried a\nverbatim copy of the kernel frame (buffer list + widths + seeded Gen,\nepilogue reads merged), the honest-window edge, and the projection\nstore with its in-kernel epilogue. One KernelFrame, one\nhonest_window_edge (the cooperative clamp is a `floor` parameter — the\none real difference the copies hid), one ProjectionStore.\n\nByte-identical llama MSL against the pre-refactor baseline (epilogue\nstores included); all 22 correctness targets green. The emitters still\ndiffer where they should: one thread per point versus the cooperative\nfold schedule.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* ir: support_below — the one down-transport, on Resolver (M-A)\n\nstream_below_{view,reindex,gather} were the support query of\nshapes_and_indexing.md hand-specialized in partition.rs, and their\ndispatch was written three times over — leaf_cuts, hot_volume, and\nstream_provenance each matched View/Reindex/Gather to call the right\none with the right resolver data. Resolver::support_below owns the\nquestion; the three walks ask it and keep only what is theirs (the\ndone-stopping, the pricing, the leaf set).\n\nFirst step of the shapes staging, delivered at its honest scope: the\nidentity query already lives as axis_refs_rc''s pass-through rule, and\ntranspose waits for its first consumer (M-C). Llama MSL byte-identical;\nall correctness targets green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* plan: name the price of one block assignment\n\nplan_axis_costed''s five nested loops buried a 90-line anonymous body\nthat computed SRAM, HBM, occupancy and time for one role assignment.\nThat body is now `price(row, col, batch, tm, tc) -> Option<f64>` —\nNone when no block of that shape fits the device — and the enumeration\nreads as what it is: every assignment, priced, first minimum wins, the\nvisiting order the tie-breaker.\n\nDeliberately NOT the iterator rework the survey imagined: tie-breaking\nrides the exact enumeration order, and an abstraction that risks it to\nsave lines fails the doc''s own test. Line-neutral (+126/−124); llama\nMSL byte-identical, so the chooser provably picks the same structures;\nlib + plan-sensitive targets green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* ir+derive: the frame transport, asserted against the arms (M-B, transitional)\n\nResolver::frame_below states the DOWN identity transport per node kind\nas data — FrameSlot::{Parent, Consumed, Broken} — and axis_aliases_via_\ntransport rewrites the alias walk as a fold over it. What stays in\nderive is exactly what is derive''s: the alias a consumed dimension\ntakes (running table, else stream, else itself) and alias_collapsed''s\nbroadcast-back under a singleton reinsertion.\n\nTransitional on purpose: the hand-written arms remain authoritative,\nthe fold runs beside them, and debug_assert_eq checks their agreement\non every graph the suite derives — it held across the whole suite on\nthe first run. The swap (delete the arms, single compute) is its own\nreviewed commit. Release behavior untouched: llama MSL byte-identical.\n\nOne truth surfaced by the factoring, now written down: the alias\ntransport is axis_refs_rc''s upward rule MINUS descriptor equality — a\nrelabeled 1:1 dimension keeps its loop variable for aliasing even\nwhere it mints a fresh occurrence upward, because reconnecting\nrelabels is what aliasing is for.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* derive: the fold is the alias table (M-B swap)\n\nThe transitional commit ran the hand-written arms beside the\nframe-transport fold and asserted their agreement on every derivation\nthe suite compiles; the assertion held everywhere. This is the other\nhalf: the arms are gone, the fold answers, and the byte-identical\nllama MSL is now a live gate — the fold''s aliases feed\nCarrier.aliases into emission, pricing, and partition.\n\naxis_aliases is 60 lines where the pair was 210: transport belongs to\nResolver::frame_below, and derive keeps only its two choices — the\nconsumed-dim alias and the broadcast-back.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* review: the write-map law in full, and every stale receipt corrected\n\nFour independent reviewers over the branch; no defect in src logic. What\nthey did find, applied:\n\n- mint now enforces all three clauses of positional_view''s law (no\n  offset, suffix-product strides, output extent = group product) — the\n  model stores coefficients where View stores groups, so consumption\n  alone left \"bijective by construction\" falsifiable. New should_panic\n  pins the underfilled-output counterexample.\n- the enumerated oracle derives summed dimensions from the relation\n  itself instead of restating the classifier''s coefficients, and the one\n  off-zoo divergence is pinned as a test: the symbolic verdict widens to\n  Dense, the direction that costs work, never correctness.\n- the §1c verify receipt pointed at a skip arm: the affine-bounds check\n  is static-only and compile-time (verify.rs:283-296), there is no\n  bind-time recheck — M-E now says built, not extended.\n- §6''s 250-350-lines-out estimate replaced by the measured truth:\n  +34 after M-A+M-B (derive -74, partition -79, ir +187), rows marked\n  landed/abandoned, projected remainder ~-130.\n- vs_tinygrad per-helper counts remeasured on the section''s own\n  code-only metric: 179 lines, 69% of the anomaly (was 205/79%).\n- frame_below''s stated law gains its missing word: minus descriptor\n  equality AND the sole-reader check (a diagonal reindex shares the\n  parent''s variable on both arms); Consumed''s doc drops the Scan case\n  the arm never produces; alias_collapsed''s doc drops the deleted\n  reference walk; contract comments stop citing stream_below_* lines\n  that no longer exist; ten node kinds, not nine.\n- honest_window_edge''s doc freezes the callers'' differing fallback\n  literal styles as preserved bytes; todo.md and CRITIQUE.md learn the\n  attic was deleted, not parked.\n\nGate: shapes_contract 8/8 (was 6), clippy --all-targets clean, fmt\nclean. src changes are doc comments only — no emitted byte can move.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"d6e77da1558cac2021144b00394a2e6661623e0a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/trees/d6e77da1558cac2021144b00394a2e6661623e0a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/commits/4a642f36a7c543daa9d956bbd915f0000d499d0a","verification":{"payload":"tree d6e77da1558cac2021144b00394a2e6661623e0a\nparent 74d63a80fbc8adf4f35599c2d135de58d367b094\nauthor Léo <44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com> 1785626157 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785626157 -0700\n\nshapes and indexing: the design, its contract, and the first two stagings (#24)\n\n* shapes and indexing: the design, and its contract in the type system\n\nThe Γ treatment applied to movement, with the tinygrad indexing/layout\npath read end to end as the foil (written up in vs_tinygrad.md):\n\n- shapes_and_indexing.md: a movement node denotes a linear correspondence\n  stored in its one affine direction (Reindex = read, View = write);\n  every structural question — six mechanisms today, two of them stating\n  contradictory laws — is a query against it. Finish ir::Resolver into\n  the one answerer; no second mechanism. Staging carries a driver per\n  step; nothing ships without one.\n\n- tests/shapes_contract.rs: the model before the implementation, as\n  gamma.rs began. Law 1 is mint privacy, Law 3 is the field list (no\n  div, no mod, no data term), Law 5 is cap() total / at(bindings)\n  partial, and the adjoint classification is a theorem: symbolic verdict\n  from coefficients equals enumerated preimage structure over the whole\n  zoo. merge.transposed() == split is a passing test, not a claim.\n\n- vs_tinygrad.md: line accounting on their own sz.py metric (sanic core\n  is 41% of theirs by token), the indexing/layout path end to end, and\n  design-debt 4 marked paid (df77288 killed the 0·Iota order trick).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* examples: delete the attic\n\n4,428 lines Cargo never compiled (no auto-discovery under a\nsubdirectory), which means never verified, which means already rotten.\nGit history keeps every line; the two living examples stay.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* shapes: the chain-depth bet, measured\n\nProbe on the real llama decode graph at ctx 1030: 2,070 nodes, 760\nmovement, maximal View/Reindex chain depth 4. Factored-on-demand walks\nstay cheap; the composed-map cache stays unbuilt.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* metal: one polarity loop where the sched emitter had six mirrors\n\nEvery sliced/unsliced pair in emit_fused_metal_sched_with iterated the\nslot partition twice with the branch inverted, differing only in\npolarity and a name prefix — the accessor closures already chose the\nper-slot names. slots_where iterates one polarity; the into/combine\npairs render through one loop over (exprs, name); the threadgroup\ncombine renders once for both polarities.\n\nNo behavior: the generated MSL for the llama decode program is\nbyte-identical, and that program exercises both polarities (the flash\nfold lane-slices its o slot against unsliced m/ℓ, including the\nmixed-accessor merge). 256 tests green across all correctness targets;\nthe measurement probes were skipped — they time GPU behavior a\nbyte-identical emitter cannot change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* metal: the two fold emitters share their frame, window, and store\n\nemit_fused_metal_with and emit_fused_metal_sched_with each carried a\nverbatim copy of the kernel frame (buffer list + widths + seeded Gen,\nepilogue reads merged), the honest-window edge, and the projection\nstore with its in-kernel epilogue. One KernelFrame, one\nhonest_window_edge (the cooperative clamp is a `floor` parameter — the\none real difference the copies hid), one ProjectionStore.\n\nByte-identical llama MSL against the pre-refactor baseline (epilogue\nstores included); all 22 correctness targets green. The emitters still\ndiffer where they should: one thread per point versus the cooperative\nfold schedule.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* ir: support_below — the one down-transport, on Resolver (M-A)\n\nstream_below_{view,reindex,gather} were the support query of\nshapes_and_indexing.md hand-specialized in partition.rs, and their\ndispatch was written three times over — leaf_cuts, hot_volume, and\nstream_provenance each matched View/Reindex/Gather to call the right\none with the right resolver data. Resolver::support_below owns the\nquestion; the three walks ask it and keep only what is theirs (the\ndone-stopping, the pricing, the leaf set).\n\nFirst step of the shapes staging, delivered at its honest scope: the\nidentity query already lives as axis_refs_rc''s pass-through rule, and\ntranspose waits for its first consumer (M-C). Llama MSL byte-identical;\nall correctness targets green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* plan: name the price of one block assignment\n\nplan_axis_costed''s five nested loops buried a 90-line anonymous body\nthat computed SRAM, HBM, occupancy and time for one role assignment.\nThat body is now `price(row, col, batch, tm, tc) -> Option<f64>` —\nNone when no block of that shape fits the device — and the enumeration\nreads as what it is: every assignment, priced, first minimum wins, the\nvisiting order the tie-breaker.\n\nDeliberately NOT the iterator rework the survey imagined: tie-breaking\nrides the exact enumeration order, and an abstraction that risks it to\nsave lines fails the doc''s own test. Line-neutral (+126/−124); llama\nMSL byte-identical, so the chooser provably picks the same structures;\nlib + plan-sensitive targets green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* ir+derive: the frame transport, asserted against the arms (M-B, transitional)\n\nResolver::frame_below states the DOWN identity transport per node kind\nas data — FrameSlot::{Parent, Consumed, Broken} — and axis_aliases_via_\ntransport rewrites the alias walk as a fold over it. What stays in\nderive is exactly what is derive''s: the alias a consumed dimension\ntakes (running table, else stream, else itself) and alias_collapsed''s\nbroadcast-back under a singleton reinsertion.\n\nTransitional on purpose: the hand-written arms remain authoritative,\nthe fold runs beside them, and debug_assert_eq checks their agreement\non every graph the suite derives — it held across the whole suite on\nthe first run. The swap (delete the arms, single compute) is its own\nreviewed commit. Release behavior untouched: llama MSL byte-identical.\n\nOne truth surfaced by the factoring, now written down: the alias\ntransport is axis_refs_rc''s upward rule MINUS descriptor equality — a\nrelabeled 1:1 dimension keeps its loop variable for aliasing even\nwhere it mints a fresh occurrence upward, because reconnecting\nrelabels is what aliasing is for.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* derive: the fold is the alias table (M-B swap)\n\nThe transitional commit ran the hand-written arms beside the\nframe-transport fold and asserted their agreement on every derivation\nthe suite compiles; the assertion held everywhere. This is the other\nhalf: the arms are gone, the fold answers, and the byte-identical\nllama MSL is now a live gate — the fold''s aliases feed\nCarrier.aliases into emission, pricing, and partition.\n\naxis_aliases is 60 lines where the pair was 210: transport belongs to\nResolver::frame_below, and derive keeps only its two choices — the\nconsumed-dim alias and the broadcast-back.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n* review: the write-map law in full, and every stale receipt corrected\n\nFour independent reviewers over the branch; no defect in src logic. What\nthey did find, applied:\n\n- mint now enforces all three clauses of positional_view''s law (no\n  offset, suffix-product strides, output extent = group product) — the\n  model stores coefficients where View stores groups, so consumption\n  alone left \"bijective by construction\" falsifiable. New should_panic\n  pins the underfilled-output counterexample.\n- the enumerated oracle derives summed dimensions from the relation\n  itself instead of restating the classifier''s coefficients, and the one\n  off-zoo divergence is pinned as a test: the symbolic verdict widens to\n  Dense, the direction that costs work, never correctness.\n- the §1c verify receipt pointed at a skip arm: the affine-bounds check\n  is static-only and compile-time (verify.rs:283-296), there is no\n  bind-time recheck — M-E now says built, not extended.\n- §6''s 250-350-lines-out estimate replaced by the measured truth:\n  +34 after M-A+M-B (derive -74, partition -79, ir +187), rows marked\n  landed/abandoned, projected remainder ~-130.\n- vs_tinygrad per-helper counts remeasured on the section''s own\n  code-only metric: 179 lines, 69% of the anomaly (was 205/79%).\n- frame_below''s stated law gains its missing word: minus descriptor\n  equality AND the sole-reader check (a diagonal reindex shares the\n  parent''s variable on both arms); Consumed''s doc drops the Scan case\n  the arm never produces; alias_collapsed''s doc drops the deleted\n  reference walk; contract comments stop citing stream_below_* lines\n  that no longer exist; ten node kinds, not nine.\n- honest_window_edge''s doc freezes the callers'' differing fallback\n  literal styles as preserved bytes; todo.md and CRITIQUE.md learn the\n  attic was deleted, not parked.\n\nGate: shapes_contract 8/8 (was 6), clippy --all-targets clean, fmt\nclean. src changes are doc comments only — no emitted byte can move.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FwNFJPm47KsKU6KJR8z4m1\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqbn4tCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAxEAQAFQIOqwG/bO5LSc+OIcqQnQn\nm5ZxgDrNY+SuBpjEa4XT2/ASlFEzdtfV5l/Qf5ZbzKVy0aXsNQCKU2CKvNNGoB+R\nT+zs+EbUIcc4hLloKEJFgR/HGlGEixxA5Gx6PWLBJH80bwOMTeR9bD2jcsG2PYtQ\nVSKFSuSrWtJg9OnZEMKfVR2DCJfusZlOJHZ4VzspeqR2WGFYDqI9gEBCV4g5VbTj\n74szTlv0NLTYSp6IRUIK6RsGt/1oPyeF9NsrLkjO/9lI4JNlwy9TtJ57tUJUb+CF\n6vk5VkwGtzrVhOwpUSuDMvIDW8E4a2S2LCe7qflnHPmRfhInes0xCpIyE6kdd1ev\nIwJokGpVcJOFA3AurCkySCQERbGTscCIUYVOn2cZVMK4ZxLXsvO9LlX+CdpEmNxu\nC+DM/Ft+wCVRHx6fqCcE/1kD0zyK2QCsJf53CkzC7WaScnwpwro5QDHvwqjzzXrs\n0SICPjLZckwJJe6jpzp5EpUDsczGganXKm0npv4ww9wAvQ8Gj5IWqrtb99JOPb2d\n9xMtmNPTpIdZS1dm9geruqVM+JMRwy6dQKrG7emr+Ub/D9xAW5Rzg4ZUDmBvP65i\n+LtLpek3HWkfLuBvpXYIpHBfwLBS95AU44Y2jCHGJ9G4ejEWp5nu7UzsUasrL6G9\nqkMb/bcYS2XJTNJMETic\n=KUFO\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-01T23:15:58Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/4a642f36a7c543daa9d956bbd915f0000d499d0a","node_id":"C_kwDOTBf03doAKDRhNjQyZjM2YTdjNTQzZGFhOWQ5NTZiYmQ5MTVmMDAwMGQ0OTlkMGE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/74d63a80fbc8adf4f35599c2d135de58d367b094","sha":"74d63a80fbc8adf4f35599c2d135de58d367b094","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/74d63a80fbc8adf4f35599c2d135de58d367b094"}],"sha":"4a642f36a7c543daa9d956bbd915f0000d499d0a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/4a642f36a7c543daa9d956bbd915f0000d499d0a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/44068562?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe","id":44068562,"login":"Leikoe","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0MDY4NTYy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/74d63a80fbc8adf4f35599c2d135de58d367b094/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-30T03:39:56Z","email":"44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Léo"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-30T03:39:56Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"number systems: what a value is, apart from how it is stored (#23)\n\n* one layout table under Dtype — widths in bits, and four facts that can no longer drift\n\nsign/exponent/significand per format, everything else computed: nbits,\nnbytes(elements) (bit-exact, div_ceil(8) — the hand-written I4 special\ncase in tensor_from_raw is deleted), is_subbyte, is_float,\nhas_infinities (not a synonym: fp8 formats are floats without them),\nexact_integers_to. Deriving the last from the table caught I8''s\noff-by-one on day one: a signed byte reaches 127, not 128.\n\nbytes() becomes bytes_per_element() and is documented as a pricing\nweight, never a size — 0.5 truncating under `as usize` is exactly how\nthe I4 special case came to exist.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* numeric: what a value IS, apart from how it is stored\n\nAn argmax index stored bf16 rounds — 14924 reloads as 14912 — because\nDtype answers \"how many bytes\" and cannot answer \"is this an index\".\nThis module answers it: NumberSystem is a chain 𝔹 ⊂ ℕ̄ ⊂ ℤ̄ ⊂ ℝ̄ ordered\nby inclusion (join is max, no lattice), Bounds carries an exact value''s\nfinite range SEPARATELY from whether ±∞ can occur, and infer derives\nboth from the graph, memoised by address the way shapes are.\n\nThe systems are extended because they must be: Min''s identity is +∞, so\nevery order fold injects a sentinel, and Const(±∞) joins as the bottom\nsystem — it is an identity element, not a real. Keeping the sentinel\nout of the finite range is what preserves \"indices ≤ 128255\" through\nargmax''s Where; the first cut collapsed the union to [0, ∞] and three\ntests caught it.\n\nmay_store is the law: exact systems must be represented exactly and\nwithin range (bf16 to 256, f16 to 2048, f32 to 2^24), reals may round,\nand a possibly-infinite value refuses every integer format. Held to the\ninterpreter in tests/numeric.rs — integrality via fract() == 0, not\ncontainment alone, because 200.5 in [0,200] passes every range check\nand still rounds wrong.\n\nNo callers yet: analysis only, the refusal comes next.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* the Γ contract, written in types, plus the design it holds\n\nnumber_systems_and_representations.md: one first-order calculus, one\ncontext, three judgments — eval/infer/emit share interp''s env shape,\nfusion is choosing a term''s let-structure, and there is no second graph\nIR (the ir.rs doctrine holds; representation facts live at names, in Γ,\nnever on terms). Records the five annotation shapes that died and the\ncounterexample that killed each.\n\ntests/contract.rs: the M12 contract as compilable Rust, run by the same\ncargo test --all-targets CI runs. Proof tokens make \"width asked before\nassigned\" unwritable; an invariant lifetime brand makes a proof from one\nΓ useless against another; Binding has no public constructor, so an\nunlawful width is unconstructible. Seven witnesses exercise the REAL\nanalysis on real graphs — argmax over the 128,256 vocab minted lawful\nunder a bf16 policy — and one is subject reduction, live against\nsimplify: simplification never widens a number system.\n\ntodo.md: M11–M16. M11''s analysis half is landed; M12 shrinks to\n\"evict Dtype from Input, Γ on Schedule\"; M13 is absorbed — buffers are\nminted at the right width, not repaired.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* ones_like: ride a constant-condition Where, not x·0 + 1\n\n−∞ · 0 = NaN, and masked tensors carry −∞ by design (attention builds\nthem with select(0, −∞)) — so ones_like returned NaN exactly where a\nmask had fired. Now the source sits on the never-taken branch of a\nWhere, contributing shape and nothing else: selection discards the\ndropped branch''s value, ±∞ and NaN included.\n\nThe old spelling derived as a count only through the 0·x → 0 peephole —\nthe same rewrite that is unsound at −∞. Its sound replacement is the\nconstant-condition fold in ewhere: Where(c≠0, a, b) = a, a law of\nselection, valid for every value it drops. tests/laws.rs holds the\nmasked lane to 1.0 and the count fold to the extent.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* refuse the boundary the storage cannot carry — M11 lands\n\nThe law wired in at the only correct moment: as partition mints a\nbuffer''s name, while the producing node is in hand unspliced with full\nIota/Coordinate/Const provenance. If the value is exact and its bounds\nexceed what the boundary dtype represents exactly, the buffer joins\nSchedule.unstorable and compile refuses the program, naming each buffer\nwith its system and bounds.\n\nNothing is widened. No dtype changes anywhere, so there is no\nproducer/consumer width to keep in agreement — the ten-site hazard that\nsank the first plan is unreachable, per \"decline, don''t guess\". An\nargmax over the 128,256-token vocabulary under --bf16 is now a loud\ncompile error instead of index 14924 silently reloading as 14912; the\nsame program at f32 compiles clean, because f32 holds the index exactly\nand the Min fold''s ±∞ identity besides. Real-valued boundaries are\nuntouched — llama still runs bf16 at full speed, and the tests assert\non the compiled schedule, so CI needs no GPU.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* structural correctness: the shape of the code, held by tests\n\nExecution oracles prove what the compiler computes; nothing proved what\nthe code IS. tests/structure.rs reads src/ as text — comments, strings\nand test modules stripped — and holds four design facts:\n\n* the expression layer (ir, derive, simplify, grad, interp, numeric, …)\n  never references the program layer (partition, plan, cost, emitters,\n  compile) — the dependency direction IS the design;\n* no core rule names a frontend operation — the standing rule, enforced\n  at zero because it measures at zero: argmax, softmax, attention and\n  friends appear only where they are defined (ir/tensor/nn) and in\n  acceptance tests;\n* there is exactly one expression IR — ir.rs''s own doctrine, pinned;\n* the debt ledger: every acknowledged design/code disagreement at its\n  EXACT count with the milestone that retires it — partition''s five\n  dev.storage reads, compile''s ten program.storage sites, graph''s four,\n  the two with_storage knobs, ir''s seven dtype mentions. Equality, not\n  a bound: growth is a caught regression, shrinkage must update the\n  ledger in the same commit, so the ledger stays the one honest list.\n\nWriting it corrected the record twice: graph.rs has four global-storage\nsites, not the three on file, and ir.rs seven dtype mentions, not a\nguessed fourteen — measured, not assumed, now pinned.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* the law mints the width the policy cannot supply — M12a\n\nstore_dtype moves from the contract file into numeric (the contract''s\nown rule 4: src converges, the file shrinks to witnesses): preferred if\nlawful, else the narrowest writable dtype that carries the value, else\nnothing. Partition calls it as each buffer is named — the one moment\nthe producing node is in hand unspliced — and records the choice in\nSchedule.minted_dtypes only when it exceeds the policy, so reals and\nsmall indices keep the boundary width and the measured win with it.\n\nSchedule::width_of is the one resolution — pin, then mint, then policy\n— read by emission''s width_of (both kernel emit sites), by consumer\nsplices (read_width at the three buffer-read sites), and through\nprogram.dtypes by allocation and readback, riding the plumbing #21\nbuilt for pinned outputs. The epilogue-rename path transfers a mint\nwith the name it protects, or writer and reader would disagree about\nexactly the width the mint exists to fix.\n\nRefusal now fires only where the law has nothing to offer: a saturated\nexact value no writable dtype carries, or a caller''s pin narrower than\nthe law (both tested). The founding defect is closed by construction\nand proven on the device: an argmax index of 3333 under a bf16 policy\nsurvives storage exactly, where the policy width would have reloaded\n3328. llama: 64.6 tok/s bf16, 53.2 f32 — untouched.\n\nStill open (M12b): evicting Dtype from Node::Input and shallow_key,\nand moving input declarations to the program layer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* a term carries no storage — M12b, the eviction\n\nNode::Input is { name, shape }: a free variable. shallow_key loses its\ndtype component, so structural identity is mathematical identity — the\nsame variable mentioned twice IS one node, and the old same-name-\ndifferent-dtype conflict is not an error case anymore, it is\nunrepresentable (its checks in verify and collect_inputs are deleted,\nand tests/tensor.rs now asserts pointer equality where it used to\nassert the refusal). input_dtypes is gone.\n\nDeclarations live where the design said they belong: Graph::input\nregisters Γ''s Caller entries; Schedule.declared_dtypes carries them;\nwidth_of resolves pin → mint → declared → policy; and every consumer —\nemission''s leaf widths, consumer splices, pricing (threaded through\nplan as Γ-resolved widths, with the quantized-weights test now stating\nits declaration as a binding: same term, three prices), the tuner,\nallocation, readback — reads that one chain. Undeclared inputs are f32,\nstated once in the chain instead of assumed at 555 call sites.\n\nPricing parity needed one care: produced buffers price at the boundary\npolicy and undeclared inputs at f32, matching what the old spliced-node\ndtypes said — the first draft defaulted both to dev.dtype_bytes and\nseven kernels appeared where two belonged.\n\nllama declares its checkpoint weights bf16 through the graph (\"the term\nis a free variable; that the checkpoint stores this weight bf16 is the\ngraph''s declaration, not the mathematics''\") and runs both modes at full\nspeed: 64.7 tok/s bf16, 53.2 f32. The structure ledger''s ir.rs entry is\nRETIRED at zero storage mentions, kept as the doctrine it became.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* DeviceSpecs, and the policy named as one — M14a\n\nDeviceProfile is DeviceSpecs: \"profile\" collided with profiling, which\nis a live concept here (SANIC_GPUTRACE, per-kernel timestamps,\nSANIC_TUNE), and the struct''s own doc promises hardware numbers. The\nboundary storage was never one — its old field comment admitted \"one\npolicy for the whole schedule\" — so it is now a typed cost::Policy the\ncaller attaches (with_storage constructs one), read through\nDeviceSpecs::storage(). The specs also gain their first capability\nfact: compute, the register width every kernel runs at, which is what\nbounds integer exactness in flight and what the demotion rule will\nconsult when assignment learns devices.\n\nwith_storage itself survives to M14-final, when the policy is handed\nto compilation directly instead of riding the specs — the structure\nledger holds both remaining sites to that milestone. §5.2''s softening\nstands: a policy default for undeclared reals is legitimate; what was\nwrong was calling it hardware.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* u32 indices: the first representation that is not a rounding — M15\n\nDtype::U32, with the layout table gaining a sign column (unsignedness\nwas inexpressible in (exponent, significand) alone): exact to 2^32-1,\nis_unsigned, saturates. Bounds now tracks WHICH infinity a value can\ncarry, because the two fold identities have opposite fates: +infinity\n(Min''s, the argmax accumulator''s start) saturates to u32::MAX by\ncontract — a defined home, not an accident — while -infinity (Max''s)\nwould alias to index 0, a plausible answer, so it refuses every integer\nformat. The pair is tested side by side.\n\nThe saturating store lives in the MSL header; its +0.5 round is exact\nrather than heuristic, because the law admits nothing wider than the\ncompute width holds exactly. U32 joins the mint''s candidates between\nf16 and f32: the argmax buffer is now the same four bytes as before and\nan INTEGER in memory, read back as one. round_to refuses U32 with the\nsentence that opens M16: stores that saturate are not a rounding of R.\n\nllama''s manual F32 pin on its token output — #21''s workaround for\nexactly this gap — retires. The law mints u32 unaided; a caller may\nstill outrank it, but no longer has to. Both modes at full speed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* the policy arrives at compilation — M14 lands whole\n\nMetalDevice is { dev, queue }: a device, nothing else. with_storage is\ngone from devices and specs alike; Backend::specs() returns hardware\nfacts, and DeviceSpecs::under(policy) is the single place hardware and\npolicy meet — called by compilation, with the Policy value the CALLER\nhanded it. Graph::compile_for takes the policy as a parameter; llama''s\n--bf16 is one line constructing Policy { boundary } and nowhere else.\nStates allocate at their own declarations rather than a device global,\nand the mismatch error now says what it means: declared vs policy.\n\nThe session''s first diagnosis was this exact category error — \"one\npolicy for the whole schedule\" living in the struct reserved for\nhardware numbers. The ledger closes the loop: graph, cost and metal\nentries retire at zero; the two survivors are reclassified as growth\nguards over policy plumbing the design licenses. En route the ledger\nalso caught its own staleness — an entry said 1 where reality said 0,\nand equality-not-bounds refused to let the disagreement stand.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* Q8: the scale is part of the type — M16 v1\n\nDtype::Q8 { scale_bits } is the first parameterised format, and it\nfired the enum-to-struct trigger exactly where the design doc predicted\nmonths of decisions ago: a quantized value is (scale, int) — the scale\nis a value, not a variant — so the type carries it, as its f32 bits,\nkeeping Copy + Eq + Hash and every Γ map untouched. MLIR''s\n!quant.uniform<i8:f32, scale> observation, one variant.\n\nThe panic that named this milestone retires. round_to(Q8) IS a rounding\n— onto the grid {q·scale : |q| ≤ 127}, clamped, idempotent like every\ndeclared rounding — implemented identically in the interpreter, the\nRust backend and Metal, so stored(q8(s)) is a value-level declaration\nall three reproduce bit-for-bit. Dequantization is the load (the scale\nbakes into the kernel as a literal); quantization is the store; and ±∞\nhave homes on the signed clamped grid — -infinity lands on -127·scale,\nunlike u32, whose floor would alias it to a plausible index. The law\nrules accordingly: reals may quantize, indices may not — a grid holds\nintegers only where its scale happens to land, so claiming any means\nclaiming the scale, and exact_integers_to(Q8) is zero.\n\nProven on the device grid-EXACT — the readback equals round_to''s grid\nby equality, not tolerance. Deliberately absent, each its own arc:\nscale inference (a calibration question, the deferred ℝ̄ policy), weight\nre-quantization, Q8 as a policy boundary.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* Γ becomes source — the contract''s types cross, sealed\n\nsrc/gamma.rs: Bindings, the context itself. Three deciders in — the\ncaller''s declarations at construction, the law''s observe at each\nbuffer''s birth, the output pins once after partition — and one\nresolution out, width_of: pin, mint, declaration, policy. The fields\nare private; append-only is asserts a caller cannot route around;\nrename carries a mint through the epilogue absorption, the case the\nmodel never knew. Schedule''s three public maps — which any caller\ncould clobber, and tests DID assign to — are gone.\n\nThe promotion found the hole it predicted: Graph::input silently\nlast-wins-overwrote a redeclaration. Declare w at bf16 then f16, and\nf16 won without a word. Now it refuses with a sentence, and the\nsame-width case is explicitly the same declaration — a shared weight\nmentioned twice, as two mentions of a variable are one node.\n\ntests/contract.rs completes its own rule 4: the toy Gamma, Bound and\nBinding are deleted; Var stays as the record of a promise kept; eight\nwitnesses run against the real types, three of them should_panic seals\nthat could not be written before because nothing refused. The module\ndoc records what deliberately did not cross — the proof token (the\ndesign has a legitimate default row) and the brand (more machinery\nthan one Bindings per compilation needs). The ledger fired downward\nonce more en route: partition''s policy reads dropped 3 to 2 as the\nmint moved into Γ.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* the design doc tells the truth at merge time\n\nStatus: implemented — every milestone landed, the defect closed by\nconstruction, the ledger holding four retired doctrines at zero. The\nstaging section''s markers follow reality, and §2''s judgment table\npoints at src/gamma.rs where it used to point at a promise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"d9fdb3f3a784b158aa1d5742a0a0b528fbd99abc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/trees/d9fdb3f3a784b158aa1d5742a0a0b528fbd99abc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/commits/74d63a80fbc8adf4f35599c2d135de58d367b094","verification":{"payload":"tree d9fdb3f3a784b158aa1d5742a0a0b528fbd99abc\nparent 32d0bf5153c8fee6364235c69d3164307a2b00f2\nauthor Léo <44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com> 1785382796 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785382796 -0700\n\nnumber systems: what a value is, apart from how it is stored (#23)\n\n* one layout table under Dtype — widths in bits, and four facts that can no longer drift\n\nsign/exponent/significand per format, everything else computed: nbits,\nnbytes(elements) (bit-exact, div_ceil(8) — the hand-written I4 special\ncase in tensor_from_raw is deleted), is_subbyte, is_float,\nhas_infinities (not a synonym: fp8 formats are floats without them),\nexact_integers_to. Deriving the last from the table caught I8''s\noff-by-one on day one: a signed byte reaches 127, not 128.\n\nbytes() becomes bytes_per_element() and is documented as a pricing\nweight, never a size — 0.5 truncating under `as usize` is exactly how\nthe I4 special case came to exist.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* numeric: what a value IS, apart from how it is stored\n\nAn argmax index stored bf16 rounds — 14924 reloads as 14912 — because\nDtype answers \"how many bytes\" and cannot answer \"is this an index\".\nThis module answers it: NumberSystem is a chain 𝔹 ⊂ ℕ̄ ⊂ ℤ̄ ⊂ ℝ̄ ordered\nby inclusion (join is max, no lattice), Bounds carries an exact value''s\nfinite range SEPARATELY from whether ±∞ can occur, and infer derives\nboth from the graph, memoised by address the way shapes are.\n\nThe systems are extended because they must be: Min''s identity is +∞, so\nevery order fold injects a sentinel, and Const(±∞) joins as the bottom\nsystem — it is an identity element, not a real. Keeping the sentinel\nout of the finite range is what preserves \"indices ≤ 128255\" through\nargmax''s Where; the first cut collapsed the union to [0, ∞] and three\ntests caught it.\n\nmay_store is the law: exact systems must be represented exactly and\nwithin range (bf16 to 256, f16 to 2048, f32 to 2^24), reals may round,\nand a possibly-infinite value refuses every integer format. Held to the\ninterpreter in tests/numeric.rs — integrality via fract() == 0, not\ncontainment alone, because 200.5 in [0,200] passes every range check\nand still rounds wrong.\n\nNo callers yet: analysis only, the refusal comes next.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* the Γ contract, written in types, plus the design it holds\n\nnumber_systems_and_representations.md: one first-order calculus, one\ncontext, three judgments — eval/infer/emit share interp''s env shape,\nfusion is choosing a term''s let-structure, and there is no second graph\nIR (the ir.rs doctrine holds; representation facts live at names, in Γ,\nnever on terms). Records the five annotation shapes that died and the\ncounterexample that killed each.\n\ntests/contract.rs: the M12 contract as compilable Rust, run by the same\ncargo test --all-targets CI runs. Proof tokens make \"width asked before\nassigned\" unwritable; an invariant lifetime brand makes a proof from one\nΓ useless against another; Binding has no public constructor, so an\nunlawful width is unconstructible. Seven witnesses exercise the REAL\nanalysis on real graphs — argmax over the 128,256 vocab minted lawful\nunder a bf16 policy — and one is subject reduction, live against\nsimplify: simplification never widens a number system.\n\ntodo.md: M11–M16. M11''s analysis half is landed; M12 shrinks to\n\"evict Dtype from Input, Γ on Schedule\"; M13 is absorbed — buffers are\nminted at the right width, not repaired.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* ones_like: ride a constant-condition Where, not x·0 + 1\n\n−∞ · 0 = NaN, and masked tensors carry −∞ by design (attention builds\nthem with select(0, −∞)) — so ones_like returned NaN exactly where a\nmask had fired. Now the source sits on the never-taken branch of a\nWhere, contributing shape and nothing else: selection discards the\ndropped branch''s value, ±∞ and NaN included.\n\nThe old spelling derived as a count only through the 0·x → 0 peephole —\nthe same rewrite that is unsound at −∞. Its sound replacement is the\nconstant-condition fold in ewhere: Where(c≠0, a, b) = a, a law of\nselection, valid for every value it drops. tests/laws.rs holds the\nmasked lane to 1.0 and the count fold to the extent.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* refuse the boundary the storage cannot carry — M11 lands\n\nThe law wired in at the only correct moment: as partition mints a\nbuffer''s name, while the producing node is in hand unspliced with full\nIota/Coordinate/Const provenance. If the value is exact and its bounds\nexceed what the boundary dtype represents exactly, the buffer joins\nSchedule.unstorable and compile refuses the program, naming each buffer\nwith its system and bounds.\n\nNothing is widened. No dtype changes anywhere, so there is no\nproducer/consumer width to keep in agreement — the ten-site hazard that\nsank the first plan is unreachable, per \"decline, don''t guess\". An\nargmax over the 128,256-token vocabulary under --bf16 is now a loud\ncompile error instead of index 14924 silently reloading as 14912; the\nsame program at f32 compiles clean, because f32 holds the index exactly\nand the Min fold''s ±∞ identity besides. Real-valued boundaries are\nuntouched — llama still runs bf16 at full speed, and the tests assert\non the compiled schedule, so CI needs no GPU.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* structural correctness: the shape of the code, held by tests\n\nExecution oracles prove what the compiler computes; nothing proved what\nthe code IS. tests/structure.rs reads src/ as text — comments, strings\nand test modules stripped — and holds four design facts:\n\n* the expression layer (ir, derive, simplify, grad, interp, numeric, …)\n  never references the program layer (partition, plan, cost, emitters,\n  compile) — the dependency direction IS the design;\n* no core rule names a frontend operation — the standing rule, enforced\n  at zero because it measures at zero: argmax, softmax, attention and\n  friends appear only where they are defined (ir/tensor/nn) and in\n  acceptance tests;\n* there is exactly one expression IR — ir.rs''s own doctrine, pinned;\n* the debt ledger: every acknowledged design/code disagreement at its\n  EXACT count with the milestone that retires it — partition''s five\n  dev.storage reads, compile''s ten program.storage sites, graph''s four,\n  the two with_storage knobs, ir''s seven dtype mentions. Equality, not\n  a bound: growth is a caught regression, shrinkage must update the\n  ledger in the same commit, so the ledger stays the one honest list.\n\nWriting it corrected the record twice: graph.rs has four global-storage\nsites, not the three on file, and ir.rs seven dtype mentions, not a\nguessed fourteen — measured, not assumed, now pinned.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* the law mints the width the policy cannot supply — M12a\n\nstore_dtype moves from the contract file into numeric (the contract''s\nown rule 4: src converges, the file shrinks to witnesses): preferred if\nlawful, else the narrowest writable dtype that carries the value, else\nnothing. 