Caller entries; Schedule.declared_dtypes carries them;\nwidth_of resolves pin → mint → declared → policy; and every consumer —\nemission''s leaf widths, consumer splices, pricing (threaded through\nplan as Γ-resolved widths, with the quantized-weights test now stating\nits declaration as a binding: same term, three prices), the tuner,\nallocation, readback — reads that one chain. Undeclared inputs are f32,\nstated once in the chain instead of assumed at 555 call sites.\n\nPricing parity needed one care: produced buffers price at the boundary\npolicy and undeclared inputs at f32, matching what the old spliced-node\ndtypes said — the first draft defaulted both to dev.dtype_bytes and\nseven kernels appeared where two belonged.\n\nllama declares its checkpoint weights bf16 through the graph (\"the term\nis a free variable; that the checkpoint stores this weight bf16 is the\ngraph''s declaration, not the mathematics''\") and runs both modes at full\nspeed: 64.7 tok/s bf16, 53.2 f32. The structure ledger''s ir.rs entry is\nRETIRED at zero storage mentions, kept as the doctrine it became.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* DeviceSpecs, and the policy named as one — M14a\n\nDeviceProfile is DeviceSpecs: \"profile\" collided with profiling, which\nis a live concept here (SANIC_GPUTRACE, per-kernel timestamps,\nSANIC_TUNE), and the struct''s own doc promises hardware numbers. The\nboundary storage was never one — its old field comment admitted \"one\npolicy for the whole schedule\" — so it is now a typed cost::Policy the\ncaller attaches (with_storage constructs one), read through\nDeviceSpecs::storage(). The specs also gain their first capability\nfact: compute, the register width every kernel runs at, which is what\nbounds integer exactness in flight and what the demotion rule will\nconsult when assignment learns devices.\n\nwith_storage itself survives to M14-final, when the policy is handed\nto compilation directly instead of riding the specs — the structure\nledger holds both remaining sites to that milestone. §5.2''s softening\nstands: a policy default for undeclared reals is legitimate; what was\nwrong was calling it hardware.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* u32 indices: the first representation that is not a rounding — M15\n\nDtype::U32, with the layout table gaining a sign column (unsignedness\nwas inexpressible in (exponent, significand) alone): exact to 2^32-1,\nis_unsigned, saturates. Bounds now tracks WHICH infinity a value can\ncarry, because the two fold identities have opposite fates: +infinity\n(Min''s, the argmax accumulator''s start) saturates to u32::MAX by\ncontract — a defined home, not an accident — while -infinity (Max''s)\nwould alias to index 0, a plausible answer, so it refuses every integer\nformat. The pair is tested side by side.\n\nThe saturating store lives in the MSL header; its +0.5 round is exact\nrather than heuristic, because the law admits nothing wider than the\ncompute width holds exactly. U32 joins the mint''s candidates between\nf16 and f32: the argmax buffer is now the same four bytes as before and\nan INTEGER in memory, read back as one. round_to refuses U32 with the\nsentence that opens M16: stores that saturate are not a rounding of R.\n\nllama''s manual F32 pin on its token output — #21''s workaround for\nexactly this gap — retires. The law mints u32 unaided; a caller may\nstill outrank it, but no longer has to. Both modes at full speed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* the policy arrives at compilation — M14 lands whole\n\nMetalDevice is { dev, queue }: a device, nothing else. with_storage is\ngone from devices and specs alike; Backend::specs() returns hardware\nfacts, and DeviceSpecs::under(policy) is the single place hardware and\npolicy meet — called by compilation, with the Policy value the CALLER\nhanded it. Graph::compile_for takes the policy as a parameter; llama''s\n--bf16 is one line constructing Policy { boundary } and nowhere else.\nStates allocate at their own declarations rather than a device global,\nand the mismatch error now says what it means: declared vs policy.\n\nThe session''s first diagnosis was this exact category error — \"one\npolicy for the whole schedule\" living in the struct reserved for\nhardware numbers. The ledger closes the loop: graph, cost and metal\nentries retire at zero; the two survivors are reclassified as growth\nguards over policy plumbing the design licenses. En route the ledger\nalso caught its own staleness — an entry said 1 where reality said 0,\nand equality-not-bounds refused to let the disagreement stand.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* Q8: the scale is part of the type — M16 v1\n\nDtype::Q8 { scale_bits } is the first parameterised format, and it\nfired the enum-to-struct trigger exactly where the design doc predicted\nmonths of decisions ago: a quantized value is (scale, int) — the scale\nis a value, not a variant — so the type carries it, as its f32 bits,\nkeeping Copy + Eq + Hash and every Γ map untouched. MLIR''s\n!quant.uniform<i8:f32, scale> observation, one variant.\n\nThe panic that named this milestone retires. round_to(Q8) IS a rounding\n— onto the grid {q·scale : |q| ≤ 127}, clamped, idempotent like every\ndeclared rounding — implemented identically in the interpreter, the\nRust backend and Metal, so stored(q8(s)) is a value-level declaration\nall three reproduce bit-for-bit. Dequantization is the load (the scale\nbakes into the kernel as a literal); quantization is the store; and ±∞\nhave homes on the signed clamped grid — -infinity lands on -127·scale,\nunlike u32, whose floor would alias it to a plausible index. The law\nrules accordingly: reals may quantize, indices may not — a grid holds\nintegers only where its scale happens to land, so claiming any means\nclaiming the scale, and exact_integers_to(Q8) is zero.\n\nProven on the device grid-EXACT — the readback equals round_to''s grid\nby equality, not tolerance. Deliberately absent, each its own arc:\nscale inference (a calibration question, the deferred ℝ̄ policy), weight\nre-quantization, Q8 as a policy boundary.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* Γ becomes source — the contract''s types cross, sealed\n\nsrc/gamma.rs: Bindings, the context itself. Three deciders in — the\ncaller''s declarations at construction, the law''s observe at each\nbuffer''s birth, the output pins once after partition — and one\nresolution out, width_of: pin, mint, declaration, policy. The fields\nare private; append-only is asserts a caller cannot route around;\nrename carries a mint through the epilogue absorption, the case the\nmodel never knew. Schedule''s three public maps — which any caller\ncould clobber, and tests DID assign to — are gone.\n\nThe promotion found the hole it predicted: Graph::input silently\nlast-wins-overwrote a redeclaration. Declare w at bf16 then f16, and\nf16 won without a word. Now it refuses with a sentence, and the\nsame-width case is explicitly the same declaration — a shared weight\nmentioned twice, as two mentions of a variable are one node.\n\ntests/contract.rs completes its own rule 4: the toy Gamma, Bound and\nBinding are deleted; Var stays as the record of a promise kept; eight\nwitnesses run against the real types, three of them should_panic seals\nthat could not be written before because nothing refused. The module\ndoc records what deliberately did not cross — the proof token (the\ndesign has a legitimate default row) and the brand (more machinery\nthan one Bindings per compilation needs). The ledger fired downward\nonce more en route: partition''s policy reads dropped 3 to 2 as the\nmint moved into Γ.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n* the design doc tells the truth at merge time\n\nStatus: implemented — every milestone landed, the defect closed by\nconstruction, the ledger holding four retired doctrines at zero. The\nstaging section''s markers follow reality, and §2''s judgment table\npoints at src/gamma.rs where it used to point at a promise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01ECzkWVgASx84EvSkR1gnbA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqaseMCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAUnYQAEYqPfy7tHO5nxL7eWkJCUvJ\nAN+Gy+sBCyJ7zjnzUsomg809kGbbvrytiS1llTDAyMtnvkrFQe2BpKL7+y9D4c/a\nEAceMllxdHbT2GhBZ32iqhkGHpXaM1QHp8bS2gP4Wl2h3QSAOWifRu8/gQWvQhoR\nmp2ZnyogBwiJM7vjoPXb/r38di2BHhFR8/dHJ68LjBEUyFMy/t9mCg2XlKTHOM3P\njtuBkkUKaAS0PU0Z7KnGhXZvnX36IvMP5S9McAvx3WwPs3Gobj+LlbyH834t2f1V\nLc3ZeDQGrZ8YjyHmLXdeicOj4BmDVfrY9BtvTcWugnTj/X18Tx5LrGI6uQkxnDNZ\nG0YbLArCujWKr/vRin/1eWA8b9ydHIrWxQMg8yMQcTQnBYcCGQXZFIqVTmB5c2pA\n2AMmuQ2u0j60ImCvCcLEfSDrs6P+MbKe2qIyMK2i57IZZLcvFchEf8Mek3Vu2O6U\nZEle3vgkdW6JQuYMuKAipd+rUWokNtry85j/4y2HF90NLNK/VvQyktn4PPIhGkwD\nIxoybnghY1qGk9lL8K3uNKxrOSJ92Yh1Kh4ZFY/ZR6bIjOpguHNBOBVBmRNgGlrZ\nzs80TCMgPUGPCrShEweasyeQXdQlGmoIhMNftuG0UyY5EQW+QgIOgvW4gzgfZdM6\nToH5DkI+KhwiJKqMf+lA\n=xAND\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-30T03:39:57Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/74d63a80fbc8adf4f35599c2d135de58d367b094","node_id":"C_kwDOTBf03doAKDc0ZDYzYTgwZmJjOGFkZjRmMzU1OTljMmQxMzVkZTU4ZDM2N2IwOTQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/32d0bf5153c8fee6364235c69d3164307a2b00f2","sha":"32d0bf5153c8fee6364235c69d3164307a2b00f2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/32d0bf5153c8fee6364235c69d3164307a2b00f2"}],"sha":"74d63a80fbc8adf4f35599c2d135de58d367b094","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/74d63a80fbc8adf4f35599c2d135de58d367b094"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/44068562?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe","id":44068562,"login":"Leikoe","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0MDY4NTYy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/32d0bf5153c8fee6364235c69d3164307a2b00f2/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-29T02:51:54Z","email":"44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Léo"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-29T02:51:54Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"encode one step ahead; document why argmax will not fuse (#22)\n\nrun_graph_timed encoded, committed and blocked, in that order, so 342 dispatch\nencodes -- 0.73 ms measured, 2.1 us each -- sat on the critical path of every\ntoken. They need not: the dispatch list and its bindings are IDENTICAL every\nstep, because a step''s inputs reach its kernels through buffer CONTENTS rather\nthan the command stream. Nothing about the encoding depends on the result being\nwaited for. So step N+1''s command buffer is built right after step N commits,\nwhile the GPU is busy.\n\nMeasured with GPU sampling (#21), balanced ABBA at ctx 1030 whose two baseline\nruns agreed to 0.17 ms:\n\n  before both   19.27 ms/tok\n  after both    18.32\n                -0.95\n\nPredicted 1.30; collected 0.95. The baseline agrees with the 19.29 measured for\nthe mlx-lm comparison, so they are the same regime: sanic 18.32 against mlx-lm''s\n18.39 at 1024. Level, from 14.6% behind.\n\nAlso documents why argmax does not fuse into lm_head, and why the obvious fix is\na trap. Coupling is discovered in exactly one place, and it is narrower than\n\"keyed on exp\" -- it is keyed on SUBTRACTION, `x - max`, whose only consumer is\nexp. Argmax couples through a comparison. Matching Where(Lt(x,max), inf, coord)\nreduced by Min would be a pattern match on argmax''s spelling: an\noperation-specific rule of the kind this repo refuses, one layer below where\nthey usually get caught. The defensible version is a family, one algebraic\nidentity per scalar primitive that can ride a running max.\n\nAnd corrects two of this document''s own traps. The measurement window has two\nedges: from cold the machine drifts 28.0 -> 19.1 getting faster, but after hours\nof sustained GPU load it throttles the other way, 18.65 -> 41.78. And\n`ps | grep cargo` finds nothing while a GPU probe saturates the device, because\na cargo test child is named after its test file -- an hour was lost blaming\noutside contention on a grep that could not have found the culprit, which was\nthis session''s own test suite.\n\n226 tests pass; both dtypes'' decode text unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"34eb39b84fb7e915640028a40bbad2407272e22c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/trees/34eb39b84fb7e915640028a40bbad2407272e22c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/commits/32d0bf5153c8fee6364235c69d3164307a2b00f2","verification":{"payload":"tree 34eb39b84fb7e915640028a40bbad2407272e22c\nparent 04eb8a9e94555c608d8b3b301bcbb56ac30b311e\nauthor Léo <44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com> 1785293514 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785293514 -0700\n\nencode one step ahead; document why argmax will not fuse (#22)\n\nrun_graph_timed encoded, committed and blocked, in that order, so 342 dispatch\nencodes -- 0.73 ms measured, 2.1 us each -- sat on the critical path of every\ntoken. They need not: the dispatch list and its bindings are IDENTICAL every\nstep, because a step''s inputs reach its kernels through buffer CONTENTS rather\nthan the command stream. Nothing about the encoding depends on the result being\nwaited for. So step N+1''s command buffer is built right after step N commits,\nwhile the GPU is busy.\n\nMeasured with GPU sampling (#21), balanced ABBA at ctx 1030 whose two baseline\nruns agreed to 0.17 ms:\n\n  before both   19.27 ms/tok\n  after both    18.32\n                -0.95\n\nPredicted 1.30; collected 0.95. The baseline agrees with the 19.29 measured for\nthe mlx-lm comparison, so they are the same regime: sanic 18.32 against mlx-lm''s\n18.39 at 1024. Level, from 14.6% behind.\n\nAlso documents why argmax does not fuse into lm_head, and why the obvious fix is\na trap. Coupling is discovered in exactly one place, and it is narrower than\n\"keyed on exp\" -- it is keyed on SUBTRACTION, `x - max`, whose only consumer is\nexp. Argmax couples through a comparison. Matching Where(Lt(x,max), inf, coord)\nreduced by Min would be a pattern match on argmax''s spelling: an\noperation-specific rule of the kind this repo refuses, one layer below where\nthey usually get caught. The defensible version is a family, one algebraic\nidentity per scalar primitive that can ride a running max.\n\nAnd corrects two of this document''s own traps. The measurement window has two\nedges: from cold the machine drifts 28.0 -> 19.1 getting faster, but after hours\nof sustained GPU load it throttles the other way, 18.65 -> 41.78. And\n`ps | grep cargo` finds nothing while a GPU probe saturates the device, because\na cargo test child is named after its test file -- an hour was lost blaming\noutside contention on a grep that could not have found the culprit, which was\nthis session''s own test suite.\n\n226 tests pass; both dtypes'' decode text unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqaWrKCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAfIkQADigQDidoqB7yxJwELCXIDRU\neHBANRF1PH2fSiFwCkZwG7oVOlu4qDkkcUToc04MlXjDYknqYOo6Q4Mnmzg5U3qe\nEGU3YaPCxFy+r+fnAfWKhBMIkBBQ/Eqyu7AidpoyX60KW8oZt/lctef1JuNituEd\nyKvggkr/NMu8digfFIXOPuRykN+QQ6P2krocmTXeu/G5EpvZZOdmoRuNvznhtd5K\n4LydkBpyPWfvFyza9g6b2zOdDSdAqNyBdDGVxYi3Bs9HlRzXoEx5hFnHgtyH7Tzx\noB92VFhoBPNvBBcp+HDTF1Me9ZR73YAfu28wmCTrk/zU/Tg+BzrYJJmZE1GUWMuZ\nRQiTXUwQ/g60u66n4qSU0vWTrGOWwzdw7BT5aZ036roLEhTrRhtK0tOzj+SAa48d\nr8aR+TUqxAKqkDlJkc9IFY94TQY0EuRR+5v66Z23SpCl4cAGFzKKTGdt7NZCA03u\nPgctJMTcghQKDx4q2X2tP+41fxvwkQb1wZ+rGhtiWUvercEd2S5AU0qotI3Q9Uch\n4cXVTV4XOTUffPRmbhW2ZHVxY+Zm1Zd3WmvdPnzzxvpmeSMN+N8AQGQq1fCqaMp2\nQLyKxddmRlpltVzP9sDz2/KeeY7lsK1WXV7hdh+/viySfftEdn0Ru0YL56KoxTkZ\n42F5e9cdTiiCfaTsGd7o\n=BoG8\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-29T02:51:54Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/32d0bf5153c8fee6364235c69d3164307a2b00f2","node_id":"C_kwDOTBf03doAKDMyZDBiZjUxNTNjOGZlZTYzNjQyMzVjNjlkMzE2NDMwN2EyYjAwZjI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/04eb8a9e94555c608d8b3b301bcbb56ac30b311e","sha":"04eb8a9e94555c608d8b3b301bcbb56ac30b311e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/04eb8a9e94555c608d8b3b301bcbb56ac30b311e"}],"sha":"32d0bf5153c8fee6364235c69d3164307a2b00f2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/32d0bf5153c8fee6364235c69d3164307a2b00f2"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/44068562?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe","id":44068562,"login":"Leikoe","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0MDY4NTYy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/04eb8a9e94555c608d8b3b301bcbb56ac30b311e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-29T01:46:54Z","email":"44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Léo"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-29T01:46:54Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"sample on the GPU, and let an output pin its own storage width (#21)\n\nGreedy sampling ran on the host: read all 128,256 logits back and scan them to\nlearn one number. Measured, that scan was 0.569 ms/tok at ctx 1030 -- more than\neither defect left in attention-long-context.md. ir::argmax already existed; the\nexample simply was not using it.\n\nWhich surfaced a real gap. An output is stored at the target''s boundary width,\nand under --bf16 that is bf16, which holds integers exactly only to 256 -- a\ntoken id past that comes back as a DIFFERENT token, and the model produced\nfluent nonsense. So Graph::output_at lets an output pin its width. This is the\nlaw the argument buffer already obeys, stated once more: values may narrow, and\nfor an activation that is the right call; addresses and indices may not, because\nthey are exact or they are wrong. Schedule carries the widths, emission stores\nat them, capture allocates and reports them. The default is unchanged.\n\nThe bf16 text changes, and the reason is recorded rather than waved at.\nir::argmax takes the FIRST maximum; the CPU''s max_by took the LAST. With 8\nmantissa bits ties are common -- at step 6 tokens 12263 and 95551 both score\nexactly 21.500000 -- and the rules part there. f32 is byte-identical, which is\nwhat shows this is tie-breaking and not a bug. First-index is what numpy, torch\nand mlx return.\n\nNot fused, and worth knowing why: argmax reads the logits twice (once for the\nmax, once for the masked index reduction), so the partitioner materializes the\nvocabulary between two folds over `vocab`. Nothing DECLINED over that axis -- the\nfolds simply never couple, because the only rule that couples a slot to a\nrunning max lives on MapOp::Exp. (max, index) is already an associative pair\nmonoid needing no repair at all, so the missing piece is discovery, not carrier\nmachinery. Closing that collapses the two folds to one pass, drops logits to a\nsingle consumer, and lets the whole thing fuse into lm_head.\n\nTests: 226 pass, plus the example''s own three including the root-shape\nassertion, which now pins one sampled index instead of a 16-entry vocabulary.\nNOT measured end to end: the machine went into heavy contention from outside\nthis session (a binary that ran 19.2 s took 98 s), so the wall-clock effect of\nremoving the 0.569 ms is still owed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"f0e2bf70313932487b6fa57cfa0449ca11db28f4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/trees/f0e2bf70313932487b6fa57cfa0449ca11db28f4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/commits/04eb8a9e94555c608d8b3b301bcbb56ac30b311e","verification":{"payload":"tree f0e2bf70313932487b6fa57cfa0449ca11db28f4\nparent 685309baf888890d533b21cf210f0fbe464183fa\nauthor Léo <44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com> 1785289614 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785289614 -0700\n\nsample on the GPU, and let an output pin its own storage width (#21)\n\nGreedy sampling ran on the host: read all 128,256 logits back and scan them to\nlearn one number. Measured, that scan was 0.569 ms/tok at ctx 1030 -- more than\neither defect left in attention-long-context.md. ir::argmax already existed; the\nexample simply was not using it.\n\nWhich surfaced a real gap. An output is stored at the target''s boundary width,\nand under --bf16 that is bf16, which holds integers exactly only to 256 -- a\ntoken id past that comes back as a DIFFERENT token, and the model produced\nfluent nonsense. So Graph::output_at lets an output pin its width. This is the\nlaw the argument buffer already obeys, stated once more: values may narrow, and\nfor an activation that is the right call; addresses and indices may not, because\nthey are exact or they are wrong. Schedule carries the widths, emission stores\nat them, capture allocates and reports them. The default is unchanged.\n\nThe bf16 text changes, and the reason is recorded rather than waved at.\nir::argmax takes the FIRST maximum; the CPU''s max_by took the LAST. With 8\nmantissa bits ties are common -- at step 6 tokens 12263 and 95551 both score\nexactly 21.500000 -- and the rules part there. f32 is byte-identical, which is\nwhat shows this is tie-breaking and not a bug. First-index is what numpy, torch\nand mlx return.\n\nNot fused, and worth knowing why: argmax reads the logits twice (once for the\nmax, once for the masked index reduction), so the partitioner materializes the\nvocabulary between two folds over `vocab`. Nothing DECLINED over that axis -- the\nfolds simply never couple, because the only rule that couples a slot to a\nrunning max lives on MapOp::Exp. (max, index) is already an associative pair\nmonoid needing no repair at all, so the missing piece is discovery, not carrier\nmachinery. Closing that collapses the two folds to one pass, drops logits to a\nsingle consumer, and lets the whole thing fuse into lm_head.\n\nTests: 226 pass, plus the example''s own three including the root-shape\nassertion, which now pins one sampled index instead of a 16-entry vocabulary.\nNOT measured end to end: the machine went into heavy contention from outside\nthis session (a binary that ran 19.2 s took 98 s), so the wall-clock effect of\nremoving the 0.569 ms is still owed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqaVuOCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAB9UQABfUDRzZD4c1ImqRtRmi154J\nnZkefF2TcHpj+BR/HUhvXU6Ffkd/uw22Tt4/jZE+bw+UIDXIreIZZyqXvO8gEOrR\nqiA06f5bdOCuGm5M7uuo02zLGCeTGkY2OYqyieStdSTL25cihxGoM/2YimCnG6el\n9BBF87Ba3Jln3F/GqU9Ip+Tu4+M4ZooRfo0FyBOv6hLFDTmqIkYYxHpL3pTCOmIt\njlCt22vIVsLBOfyJ258/+pJbwRGpGE/fBndPR9MvyGgExmwtI4EEv14IDC9yiwQa\niR1BOfQTnHeW2xQyeYce9HE5Ho8x0WgZWdtnd/0vx1EY48FoekWZ8AJuLHG5GVZD\nVWhmvkGucxOgDYdcHrD6nGsoh3BMraK49qXWTPMhzyex44VaAJh9gnjyDvbCA1B/\n7Gy1IlyJ+gJl1abStc/XYle+4d1EhwdzzruZ1lWrzZxQp5ptLeRJlGz+wluxw6e+\nKqlLTuGqpj0s8s9TM5MYasTOmavbx55dVLMS6ni3tbDAeSPJaWSVZAgWtuisl0qT\nGwexr5d9Bf77xD6V5i4HMRmc5k3Z3ecES94t9Q8O398rNi5+BMTBGU10hgt60KHr\nj42QIkClph0czwfQtomu/b2Gpb16k8jdZHGoiPPi4ui2kgJ1V+UaUEAwYWk5cPIG\n4LYXFsr3xgmBqCKsRh1+\n=Opj2\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-29T01:46:55Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/04eb8a9e94555c608d8b3b301bcbb56ac30b311e","node_id":"C_kwDOTBf03doAKDA0ZWI4YTllOTQ1NTVjNjA4ZDhiM2IzMDFiY2JiNTZhYzMwYjMxMWU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/685309baf888890d533b21cf210f0fbe464183fa","sha":"685309baf888890d533b21cf210f0fbe464183fa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/685309baf888890d533b21cf210f0fbe464183fa"}],"sha":"04eb8a9e94555c608d8b3b301bcbb56ac30b311e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/04eb8a9e94555c608d8b3b301bcbb56ac30b311e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/44068562?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe","id":44068562,"login":"Leikoe","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0MDY4NTYy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/685309baf888890d533b21cf210f0fbe464183fa/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-29T01:46:25Z","email":"44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Léo"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-29T01:46:25Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"attention-long-context: re-measured against mlx-lm, and the gap left is host-side (#20)\n\nThe document''s headline predated both fixes AND the warm-up discipline, so it\nwas stale twice over. Re-run in one warm session, ABBA per length:\n\n  n      sanic   mlx-lm\n  32     15.78   16.49    sanic ahead 4.3%\n  128    16.80   17.48    sanic ahead 3.9%\n  512    18.39   18.42    tie\n  1024   19.29   18.39    mlx ahead 4.9%   (n=8 each)\n\nThe 1024 gap went from 14.6% to 4.9% and the crossover moved from between\n32-128 out to between 512-1024. Slope 32 -> 1024 is 3.43 against mlx''s 1.90 --\nstill worse, 1.8x rather than 2.5x.\n\nThe more useful half: sanic''s GPU replay at 1024 is 17.0 ms on every one of the\neight runs, which is 1.4 ms FASTER than mlx-lm''s entire wall clock. Everything\nwe are behind by is host-side. Instrumented, and it sums to the wall clock:\n\n  GPU replay                        17.0  ms\n  step() wall - GPU                  1.908\n  CPU argmax over 128,256 logits     0.569\n  read + widen the logits buffer     0.040\n  everything else                    0.062\n\nHost overhead is now the largest single item left -- 2.6 ms/tok against\n~0.4-0.7 for defect C and <=0.4 for B''s grid half. The 1.9 ms sits around the\nreplay: the host commits the frozen graph and blocks. 342 dispatches x the\n4.2 us per-dispatch cost from applegpu/bandwidth.md is 1.44 ms, close enough\nthat it is worth testing whether this is per-dispatch -- if it is, fewer kernels\nis the lever, not a faster host loop.\n\nBoth columns moved between the two tables, since the first predates the warm-up\ndiscipline. Only the within-session comparison means anything, and the document\nsays so.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"8e39a9d95f36d5b6b18b1a4d5b0fecf36f570b9f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/trees/8e39a9d95f36d5b6b18b1a4d5b0fecf36f570b9f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/commits/685309baf888890d533b21cf210f0fbe464183fa","verification":{"payload":"tree 8e39a9d95f36d5b6b18b1a4d5b0fecf36f570b9f\nparent b9ecdbb7c3eb835701e836b7a1d8dca37269555f\nauthor Léo <44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com> 1785289585 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785289585 -0700\n\nattention-long-context: re-measured against mlx-lm, and the gap left is host-side (#20)\n\nThe document''s headline predated both fixes AND the warm-up discipline, so it\nwas stale twice over. Re-run in one warm session, ABBA per length:\n\n  n      sanic   mlx-lm\n  32     15.78   16.49    sanic ahead 4.3%\n  128    16.80   17.48    sanic ahead 3.9%\n  512    18.39   18.42    tie\n  1024   19.29   18.39    mlx ahead 4.9%   (n=8 each)\n\nThe 1024 gap went from 14.6% to 4.9% and the crossover moved from between\n32-128 out to between 512-1024. Slope 32 -> 1024 is 3.43 against mlx''s 1.90 --\nstill worse, 1.8x rather than 2.5x.\n\nThe more useful half: sanic''s GPU replay at 1024 is 17.0 ms on every one of the\neight runs, which is 1.4 ms FASTER than mlx-lm''s entire wall clock. Everything\nwe are behind by is host-side. Instrumented, and it sums to the wall clock:\n\n  GPU replay                        17.0  ms\n  step() wall - GPU                  1.908\n  CPU argmax over 128,256 logits     0.569\n  read + widen the logits buffer     0.040\n  everything else                    0.062\n\nHost overhead is now the largest single item left -- 2.6 ms/tok against\n~0.4-0.7 for defect C and <=0.4 for B''s grid half. The 1.9 ms sits around the\nreplay: the host commits the frozen graph and blocks. 342 dispatches x the\n4.2 us per-dispatch cost from applegpu/bandwidth.md is 1.44 ms, close enough\nthat it is worth testing whether this is per-dispatch -- if it is, fewer kernels\nis the lever, not a faster host loop.\n\nBoth columns moved between the two tables, since the first predates the warm-up\ndiscipline. Only the within-session comparison means anything, and the document\nsays so.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqaVtxCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAxgMQAJ0E+Jvvc7iseIX0lvedH+nV\n1q+vynQTSSBrL8D7RHwAjcaMy39FGiBHggII+3fuXn8VJ6pQV1ZEQx8S36PSESW+\nSUSZvfOMIhpJ5t+w1ADSyjenpluQDhMqAystR8gRYvcATwpBZFuhaLUH1zSTNlws\nqw55H8bOWwiPK7kr7qLqjOry2GmQqDz5UiCSOj9QEKLZBfueUQIIZUd8dM6cxC16\ni4pX1W7i7maijxHH+paSmYX+XzvSu9JbMYV1z2EsGVrfdOzLBB8tz6zT9cSO+0AN\nxOmDytRtvxDhTpH2Vx09Y9kj/sHMTTJpmftryjZ7zs2kUQ6R0cdmlVwMHs7EvAB9\nYe/BVU/EO5kIPa+2Jz1wuyRivrm0QJdqS+ZNdEcUnn5CenUGQcwvbZ5yNmF8ZEeG\nxwu9RBgNA2nxirkY/9xoz2wlmkQQlq9Ppqe4aaCAhMVG5E4TLqk2PYAi7eTZPnz2\npD/digVr6GtUuMDk1uUbWMMVrr9f5o2uOcUnNfqbmdlIPbSu3NeIfdNZpL6F8L3m\nz9/1xMm4rafDXKAdn2wMHKWT3GNPUB3zWYiWAEmvTPlw9GZvDYDrscp9+vrac1j+\nrqM0m70ozSfnPMhe+F2bXou/WNa1IAVLsYa/FM/qtnWxZDp63tk6QCBlxtGbNb2Z\n5ixBdbYeUEAQmDdVNV45\n=qbvA\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-29T01:46:26Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/685309baf888890d533b21cf210f0fbe464183fa","node_id":"C_kwDOTBf03doAKDY4NTMwOWJhZjg4ODg5MGQ1MzNiMjFjZjIxMGYwZmJlNDY0MTgzZmE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/b9ecdbb7c3eb835701e836b7a1d8dca37269555f","sha":"b9ecdbb7c3eb835701e836b7a1d8dca37269555f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/b9ecdbb7c3eb835701e836b7a1d8dca37269555f"}],"sha":"685309baf888890d533b21cf210f0fbe464183fa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/685309baf888890d533b21cf210f0fbe464183fa"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/44068562?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe","id":44068562,"login":"Leikoe","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0MDY4NTYy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/b9ecdbb7c3eb835701e836b7a1d8dca37269555f/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-28T22:47:40Z","email":"44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Léo"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-28T22:47:40Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"applegpu/metal: a downloader, not a copy of Apple''s docs (#19)\n\nApple''s Metal reference is copyrighted and not ours to redistribute, so what is\nchecked in is the script that fetches it and a .gitignore that keeps everything\nit writes out of git. Run it and the tree appears beside it:\n\n    python3 applegpu/metal/dl.py\n\n3,835 pages in 57 seconds, nothing missing.\n\ndeveloper.apple.com is a JS app, so fetching a documentation URL gets you a\ntitle and nothing else. It is backed by the same DocC render JSON the page\nitself consumes, which carries the abstract, declarations, parameters,\ndiscussion, code listings, and a topicSections list naming each page''s\nchildren -- so the tree is walkable. dl.py walks it breadth-first and mirrors\nthe URL hierarchy: a page with children becomes a directory holding index.md, a\nleaf becomes <name>.md.\n\nRound-trip latency is the entire cost, so the fetches overlap: one at a time\nmanages under a page per second, two dozen in flight do about sixty-seven.\nStdlib only -- the blocking fetch runs in asyncio''s thread pool, which is all\nthis workload needs and keeps it a single file with no install step. Re-running\nskips what is on disk, so an interrupted run resumes.\n\nAlso here, the other half of the task: gpudebug, which both of the Apple docs we\nhad listed as unmined describe, is a text REPL over .gputrace files with a\n`performance` subtree -- the terminal route to per-dispatch counters that\ncurrently need the Xcode GUI, against traces we already emit via SANIC_GPUTRACE.\n\nIt does not exist on this machine. Measured at macOS 26.5.2 (25F84) and Xcode\n26.6 (17F113), both NEWER than the macOS 26.2 in Apple''s own example: not on\nPATH, not an xcrun utility, no man page, and absent from the whole Xcode.app and\n/Library/Developer trees. Recorded in counters.md as a measured absence, with\neverything about what it would show labelled as unverified vendor documentation.\nWorth knowing for whoever gets it working: every documented example is render\nwork, so whether a compute-only trace exposes anything under `performance` is\nitself unknown.\n\nThe corpus is documentation, not measurement. Nothing fetched into metal/\nbelongs in the five measured files without a number from this machine beside it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"b43396da8cf3421cd05a099e35947521b5a2d38d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/trees/b43396da8cf3421cd05a099e35947521b5a2d38d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/commits/b9ecdbb7c3eb835701e836b7a1d8dca37269555f","verification":{"payload":"tree b43396da8cf3421cd05a099e35947521b5a2d38d\nparent 7831b4b233e702eeb5fa4f4c7792f9f3c71d69a0\nauthor Léo <44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com> 1785278860 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785278860 -0700\n\napplegpu/metal: a downloader, not a copy of Apple''s docs (#19)\n\nApple''s Metal reference is copyrighted and not ours to redistribute, so what is\nchecked in is the script that fetches it and a .gitignore that keeps everything\nit writes out of git. Run it and the tree appears beside it:\n\n    python3 applegpu/metal/dl.py\n\n3,835 pages in 57 seconds, nothing missing.\n\ndeveloper.apple.com is a JS app, so fetching a documentation URL gets you a\ntitle and nothing else. It is backed by the same DocC render JSON the page\nitself consumes, which carries the abstract, declarations, parameters,\ndiscussion, code listings, and a topicSections list naming each page''s\nchildren -- so the tree is walkable. dl.py walks it breadth-first and mirrors\nthe URL hierarchy: a page with children becomes a directory holding index.md, a\nleaf becomes <name>.md.\n\nRound-trip latency is the entire cost, so the fetches overlap: one at a time\nmanages under a page per second, two dozen in flight do about sixty-seven.\nStdlib only -- the blocking fetch runs in asyncio''s thread pool, which is all\nthis workload needs and keeps it a single file with no install step. Re-running\nskips what is on disk, so an interrupted run resumes.\n\nAlso here, the other half of the task: gpudebug, which both of the Apple docs we\nhad listed as unmined describe, is a text REPL over .gputrace files with a\n`performance` subtree -- the terminal route to per-dispatch counters that\ncurrently need the Xcode GUI, against traces we already emit via SANIC_GPUTRACE.\n\nIt does not exist on this machine. Measured at macOS 26.5.2 (25F84) and Xcode\n26.6 (17F113), both NEWER than the macOS 26.2 in Apple''s own example: not on\nPATH, not an xcrun utility, no man page, and absent from the whole Xcode.app and\n/Library/Developer trees. Recorded in counters.md as a measured absence, with\neverything about what it would show labelled as unverified vendor documentation.\nWorth knowing for whoever gets it working: every documented example is render\nwork, so whether a compute-only trace exposes anything under `performance` is\nitself unknown.\n\nThe corpus is documentation, not measurement. Nothing fetched into metal/\nbelongs in the five measured files without a number from this machine beside it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqaTGMCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAdGwQAJMDbwO6UfLS2OZWfxfKjANF\n8lzBIr+JEOP0yx7ESQZvBn8FhTD8/tBA4UQYCru9cusat8pauuPZFe4DQmwpAchu\nJfd2v0N5rapdlU8vEQBmjSyWDq6b8f0To2mBzwvPtj0aPrJXDB5VCsUz5uPKSp91\n2yixt5CISPrMbrvd1AK/+py2luuhr7pQSCg+hJ9tqKP5zic7FV98ZqO5PkYolUFi\nNqRFUUPpG4fSoXQVMIKB+DFDKCQZyymvX8QjDtL/vYF6LgfnX0d0BArXGrfU6qB5\nK37TX/vdOUzW5tTTDszD3LirUdBVG11baaPzghZDgdJmBG/etIWLw50zcF4/iMDZ\nj5APlj8Ib+752kBd/PODgFOu70SzUgqXspm5gdXbANnIFjXSH//s/tNY7shhi0L0\nfMgTAbqpUXJFW56z4WtBMmM+Zs78Bo/3k6Fsfqo9bUHztx3cm3J1mAqQK2Ug/qTe\nLk8XY3uvCUynwg6KUNXPJw08imrKVjtvL7NkVyZ8yrqyjhjjWiWdh+h8pXcmj7W5\nERcSQ4T7s1Mtx/2PCrSag1jNlrQ3c4//N19eIM7+AJHnrdvUpGqdTAieIpJCERyq\n/UW4n10AhNescH6OrdArPBazpD262/GbJbXXVhZkm0UGnJrD/AeHlQO0kRWyHzEG\njrqWwlxAlX6+P/xzZGqS\n=2+RB\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-28T22:47:41Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/b9ecdbb7c3eb835701e836b7a1d8dca37269555f","node_id":"C_kwDOTBf03doAKGI5ZWNkYmI3YzNlYjgzNTcwMWU4MzZiN2ExZDhkY2EzNzI2OTU1NWY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/7831b4b233e702eeb5fa4f4c7792f9f3c71d69a0","sha":"7831b4b233e702eeb5fa4f4c7792f9f3c71d69a0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/7831b4b233e702eeb5fa4f4c7792f9f3c71d69a0"}],"sha":"b9ecdbb7c3eb835701e836b7a1d8dca37269555f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/b9ecdbb7c3eb835701e836b7a1d8dca37269555f"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/44068562?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe","id":44068562,"login":"Leikoe","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0MDY4NTYy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/7831b4b233e702eeb5fa4f4c7792f9f3c71d69a0/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-28T21:55:12Z","email":"44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Léo"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-28T21:55:12Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"each cache is stored with the axis its fold contracts innermost (#17)\n\nThe output fold walked V at a 128-byte stride: V[kv*65920 + s*64 + d], folded\nover s. Lanes take consecutive s, so ONE 32-lane load touched 32 distinct cache\nlines -- 4096 bytes fetched to use 64.\n\nNothing about that is inherent to the fold; it is where V sits. K is contracted\nover head_dim by the scores fold and wants head_dim innermost. V is contracted\nover cache_sequence by the output fold and wants cache_sequence innermost. They\nare different tensors and may have different layouts, so V is now stored\n[kv_heads, head_dim, cache_sequence] and the load becomes contiguous.\n\nThe transpose FUSES into the fold -- same 342 kernels, nothing materialized --\nbecause a view only changes the offsets codegen computes. The one API change is\nupdate_cache taking the sequence dimension instead of assuming dim 1: which\ndimension a cache counts positions along is a layout choice, and layout is what\ndecides whether the folds that read it stride.\n\nMeasured warm, ABBA x4 back-to-back, n=8 each, at ctx 1030:\n\n  main (B fixed)   18.91 ms/tok   (18.8 - 19.0)\n  V transposed     17.08 ms/tok   (17.0 - 17.2)\n\n-1.84 ms/tok, pooled sd 0.07, ranges disjoint. Output fold class 4454 -> 801 us\n(5.6x); scores fold flat at 1891 (K untouched). Slope over 32 -> 1024 is halved:\n+3.90 before, +3.71 after B, +1.88 after both. Nothing changes at ctx 38.\n\nThis refutes two things the document said, both earned by measurement:\n\n  - The transpose trap said transposing \"moves the problem\" because it makes the\n    cache write strided. True when written; false once B was fixed, because the\n    write is now 512 threads and V''s scatter costs 391 us against K''s contiguous\n    432. Fixing B is what made A''s fix free. The order mattered.\n  - The proposed fix for A was to stage a V tile in threadgroup memory. That\n    would have meant building a tiling capability sanic does not have: the\n    emitter is one thread per output point, FoldSched schedules the reduction,\n    threadgroup memory only merges partials, and no emitter reads a tile field.\n    With thread count pinned by the 0.61x trap and no tiling, layout is the only\n    lever.\n\nAlso recorded: the debug->production ratio is not constant -- x0.50 for A, x0.31\nfor B, against the x0.735 assumed. C''s 1.41 ms is relabelled an upper bound.\n\n226 tests pass. bf16 text byte-identical at 32 and at 1024 (md5), f32 default\nbyte-identical.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"95a44171769dcb8e0e37a60c69562a016848cc94","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/trees/95a44171769dcb8e0e37a60c69562a016848cc94"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/commits/7831b4b233e702eeb5fa4f4c7792f9f3c71d69a0","verification":{"payload":"tree 95a44171769dcb8e0e37a60c69562a016848cc94\nparent cda41dc1438ae2d6caf098dcc31389d2aa835b54\nauthor Léo <44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com> 1785275712 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785275712 -0700\n\neach cache is stored with the axis its fold contracts innermost (#17)\n\nThe output fold walked V at a 128-byte stride: V[kv*65920 + s*64 + d], folded\nover s. Lanes take consecutive s, so ONE 32-lane load touched 32 distinct cache\nlines -- 4096 bytes fetched to use 64.\n\nNothing about that is inherent to the fold; it is where V sits. K is contracted\nover head_dim by the scores fold and wants head_dim innermost. V is contracted\nover cache_sequence by the output fold and wants cache_sequence innermost. They\nare different tensors and may have different layouts, so V is now stored\n[kv_heads, head_dim, cache_sequence] and the load becomes contiguous.\n\nThe transpose FUSES into the fold -- same 342 kernels, nothing materialized --\nbecause a view only changes the offsets codegen computes. The one API change is\nupdate_cache taking the sequence dimension instead of assuming dim 1: which\ndimension a cache counts positions along is a layout choice, and layout is what\ndecides whether the folds that read it stride.\n\nMeasured warm, ABBA x4 back-to-back, n=8 each, at ctx 1030:\n\n  main (B fixed)   18.91 ms/tok   (18.8 - 19.0)\n  V transposed     17.08 ms/tok   (17.0 - 17.2)\n\n-1.84 ms/tok, pooled sd 0.07, ranges disjoint. Output fold class 4454 -> 801 us\n(5.6x); scores fold flat at 1891 (K untouched). Slope over 32 -> 1024 is halved:\n+3.90 before, +3.71 after B, +1.88 after both. Nothing changes at ctx 38.\n\nThis refutes two things the document said, both earned by measurement:\n\n  - The transpose trap said transposing \"moves the problem\" because it makes the\n    cache write strided. True when written; false once B was fixed, because the\n    write is now 512 threads and V''s scatter costs 391 us against K''s contiguous\n    432. Fixing B is what made A''s fix free. The order mattered.\n  - The proposed fix for A was to stage a V tile in threadgroup memory. That\n    would have meant building a tiling capability sanic does not have: the\n    emitter is one thread per output point, FoldSched schedules the reduction,\n    threadgroup memory only merges partials, and no emitter reads a tile field.\n    With thread count pinned by the 0.61x trap and no tiling, layout is the only\n    lever.\n\nAlso recorded: the debug->production ratio is not constant -- x0.50 for A, x0.31\nfor B, against the x0.735 assumed. C''s 1.41 ms is relabelled an upper bound.\n\n226 tests pass. bf16 text byte-identical at 32 and at 1024 (md5), f32 default\nbyte-identical.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqaSVACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAey8QAG4gHqGr1a3/vXvnQ61HeFOH\nyw5+TgaA+B62THk6VgA/Qm7oGjQi1mO95YRc1ENYA3HSdGYMvpl8GJY0mlF+2LMu\nayRJTpSQSh1crLfJFBNTG97utNZJKsc2p03Aynw47jlsCFSYlgEk1z2HYcMPjGaD\nmXd/sWFvp7xTukwwRwROEYrotCe7DY5maxz4QGuo3G+czfKlHT2eZgMPwl5qUH4n\nMnjiXUs2s1PT/kVC2R8SpmGR+lo+yx1Sp8dIxmXKlJF0uOVScWRaD3gk93V0XLLo\nuqv7kiAApSubsdiHQ1myXnZpIeKlQ4NnLoQYZ/UaeSIkF/jyHQ/8jlUfhmm8XuQt\nsX6qHvhGIq6VDuFxbIfq5WHQV46nZ7Zh3lSqVq58/YRR+dqxkVMN/K6dEjtasjlV\n+hfg1AvPAeHko4j5MArcCjuM1Wv1bUww4xn4xwT7lK4d+XKdNzfMneVlYi7kNEuY\nF3cHLpTMghxaF8VG5/McRoch7mKeU1quNV8ycvXk82vH5umPZBCuO55Resfr3p6U\nkikboM04lwh4AHuq3eEoqyWi26NdtAuDRvcM3kO4KdpDI53/p/7qrIhm0kSCU+DK\nUuzjYzv/HTgr6V6q+KClfY0YAMrCqAHGNhbdBtQKLmrUTI3hkP+0XzZd33THdPl9\nL4075xibVpL3V3xwm78Z\n=1q31\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-28T21:55:12Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/7831b4b233e702eeb5fa4f4c7792f9f3c71d69a0","node_id":"C_kwDOTBf03doAKDc4MzFiNGIyMzNlNzAyZWViNWZhNGY0Yzc3OTJmOWYzYzcxZDY5YTA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/cda41dc1438ae2d6caf098dcc31389d2aa835b54","sha":"cda41dc1438ae2d6caf098dcc31389d2aa835b54","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/cda41dc1438ae2d6caf098dcc31389d2aa835b54"}],"sha":"7831b4b233e702eeb5fa4f4c7792f9f3c71d69a0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/7831b4b233e702eeb5fa4f4c7792f9f3c71d69a0"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/44068562?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe","id":44068562,"login":"Leikoe","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0MDY4NTYy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/cda41dc1438ae2d6caf098dcc31389d2aa835b54/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-28T20:15:18Z","email":"44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Léo"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-28T20:15:18Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"the KV cache is written in place, not copied (#16)\n\n`where(c, x, s)` differs from `s` only where `c` is nonzero. So when a\npersistent state''s successor has that shape and `s` is the state''s own input,\nthe successor IS the old state with a part replaced — and it can be written\ninto `s`''s buffer, because the points it does not store already hold the right\nvalues.\n\nLlama''s decode step was exactly this: 32 cache updates, each mapping all\n8 x 1030 x 64 positions to change one, 67.5 MB of read+write per step to\ndeliver 32 KB. The rest was a copy from one ping-pong buffer to the other.\n\nThree pieces, no new IR:\n\n  - Graph::overwritable_states states the law and checks its one precondition —\n    that nobody still wants the old contents. Counting settles it: the step\n    reads the state''s values exactly once, in the fallback arm the law erases.\n    A Coordinate''s source does not count, being a function of shape, not\n    contents.\n  - compile_roots_in_place carries the claim to emission through\n    Schedule::agrees_in_place, where the kernel evaluates the condition, retires\n    the threads that fail it, and never loads the state at all.\n  - capture binds the pair as one buffer instead of two that trade roles, so the\n    ping-pong disappears: one parity, one graph, one allocation.\n\nMeasured, ABBA x4 back-to-back in one warm session, n=8 each: 19.18 -> 18.94\nms/tok at ctx 1030, pooled sd 0.06, ranges non-overlapping. Unchanged at ctx 38.\nThe per-kernel class fell 2402 -> 1630 us with every other class flat.\n\nThe document predicted 0.78 ms and got 0.24, and that miss is the more valuable\nresult. The predictions scaled SANIC_DEBUG=4 deltas by 0.735 — the ratio of\nTOTALS — while the observed delta ratio is ~0.31, because in production one\nconcurrent encoder overlaps these dispatches with other work. A''s 3.45 ms and\nC''s 1.41 ms are relabelled upper bounds (~1.5 and ~0.6 expected). A is still the\nwhole ballgame.\n\nTwo measurement traps recorded, both of which cost time: the machine needs\nminutes of sustained load to reach its fast state and idle gaps give it back (a\n\"cooled\" 8-run ABBA drifted 28.0 -> 19.1 ms/tok on the same binary, measuring\nthe warm-up rather than the change), and absolute numbers in that document do\nnot survive across sessions.\n\nWhat remains of B is the grid, which still covers the full extent — 515\nthreadgroups to write 512 values. Closing it needs a domain restriction rather\nthan a store guard.\n\n226 tests pass. bf16 and the f32 default both byte-identical to a baseline\nbinary built from the parent commit. Backend::execute now refuses a program\ncompiled this way, since only capture binds the alias.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"1bc132797cdc669e561aa28774ad854a1c876bbc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/trees/1bc132797cdc669e561aa28774ad854a1c876bbc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/commits/cda41dc1438ae2d6caf098dcc31389d2aa835b54","verification":{"payload":"tree 1bc132797cdc669e561aa28774ad854a1c876bbc\nparent edfc5e1c5ff846ab473882b51174cc61a58e3488\nauthor Léo <44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com> 1785269718 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785269718 -0700\n\nthe KV cache is written in place, not copied (#16)\n\n`where(c, x, s)` differs from `s` only where `c` is nonzero. So when a\npersistent state''s successor has that shape and `s` is the state''s own input,\nthe successor IS the old state with a part replaced — and it can be written\ninto `s`''s buffer, because the points it does not store already hold the right\nvalues.\n\nLlama''s decode step was exactly this: 32 cache updates, each mapping all\n8 x 1030 x 64 positions to change one, 67.5 MB of read+write per step to\ndeliver 32 KB. The rest was a copy from one ping-pong buffer to the other.\n\nThree pieces, no new IR:\n\n  - Graph::overwritable_states states the law and checks its one precondition —\n    that nobody still wants the old contents. Counting settles it: the step\n    reads the state''s values exactly once, in the fallback arm the law erases.\n    A Coordinate''s source does not count, being a function of shape, not\n    contents.\n  - compile_roots_in_place carries the claim to emission through\n    Schedule::agrees_in_place, where the kernel evaluates the condition, retires\n    the threads that fail it, and never loads the state at all.\n  - capture binds the pair as one buffer instead of two that trade roles, so the\n    ping-pong disappears: one parity, one graph, one allocation.\n\nMeasured, ABBA x4 back-to-back in one warm session, n=8 each: 19.18 -> 18.94\nms/tok at ctx 1030, pooled sd 0.06, ranges non-overlapping. Unchanged at ctx 38.\nThe per-kernel class fell 2402 -> 1630 us with every other class flat.\n\nThe document predicted 0.78 ms and got 0.24, and that miss is the more valuable\nresult. The predictions scaled SANIC_DEBUG=4 deltas by 0.735 — the ratio of\nTOTALS — while the observed delta ratio is ~0.31, because in production one\nconcurrent encoder overlaps these dispatches with other work. A''s 3.45 ms and\nC''s 1.41 ms are relabelled upper bounds (~1.5 and ~0.6 expected). A is still the\nwhole ballgame.\n\nTwo measurement traps recorded, both of which cost time: the machine needs\nminutes of sustained load to reach its fast state and idle gaps give it back (a\n\"cooled\" 8-run ABBA drifted 28.0 -> 19.1 ms/tok on the same binary, measuring\nthe warm-up rather than the change), and absolute numbers in that document do\nnot survive across sessions.\n\nWhat remains of B is the grid, which still covers the full extent — 515\nthreadgroups to write 512 values. Closing it needs a domain restriction rather\nthan a store guard.\n\n226 tests pass. bf16 and the f32 default both byte-identical to a baseline\nbinary built from the parent commit. Backend::execute now refuses a program\ncompiled this way, since only capture binds the alias.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqaQ3WCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAApRsQACMEiPC0bma62GseVBzsk+rW\nCDqGa9F4neZyMlNkUgnB7g47J7OBUSgzueHRhDIhCyD+8cix+2ku6ktYF2KVdo4l\nCnG3iuLBI9RxyC619HP5hiELwbJRKlWH+fPUEaOXtZfLfSwPhMXlhyMynd0f7LBH\ngrO6RCUAaJEthoxPMBiyNHereEqKHkuKzI5UZwWKIr2kHQJRdjxZbYb/se0S+iHk\nnLUV6+16tKLmEEe1oo24TD9Axkzpi7vWJU166nQJvCTVvkMMMYjOw9din2yVRyPe\nEdQ4dHbIN+Jk/GowXhavYzHLiWILpmcc9W209JdUqRSchXeLivHQWNrXqAayoFPi\nV36+kMOx0oGwXujsjzcIvMzOdMfE4hyPIyz7h1il35eFjUoWcyxTvN2kC0m4hoqN\no/yqM57JanGsIL2wKSVGJKtJ+Bl9x/euFyG+f45XPUwbly/uFfNtYQM+hkPj7ZTp\nLtz3Bpyn920+WyqayVay1BYNC3/9+WyBmXgMgkMYDByDz3DFtyRfeaGyAjrlChe1\n57nQaR36gMnCLhw0yU4DxaS+t0QoarNSbHhatHaFpMZ23efH8bS60eSjqAMb8VRz\nS2iUNTUqF9frsU3BVHmiK2RatCZN23pQRi4n2X64wE/BDg5EHVqEvx8tGQNacjyD\n0XRQhqxAKnhUYks19+30\n=6pBB\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-28T20:15:19Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/cda41dc1438ae2d6caf098dcc31389d2aa835b54","node_id":"C_kwDOTBf03doAKGNkYTQxZGMxNDM4YWUyZDZjYWYwOThkY2MzMTM4OWQyYWE4MzViNTQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/edfc5e1c5ff846ab473882b51174cc61a58e3488","sha":"edfc5e1c5ff846ab473882b51174cc61a58e3488","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/edfc5e1c5ff846ab473882b51174cc61a58e3488"}],"sha":"cda41dc1438ae2d6caf098dcc31389d2aa835b54","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/cda41dc1438ae2d6caf098dcc31389d2aa835b54"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/44068562?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe","id":44068562,"login":"Leikoe","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0MDY4NTYy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/edfc5e1c5ff846ab473882b51174cc61a58e3488/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-28T16:31:16Z","email":"44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Léo"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-28T16:31:16Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"attention doesn''t scale with context: the whole 1024-token gap, in three defects (#15)\n\nMeasured sanic vs mlx-lm at 32/128/512/1024 generated tokens, ABBA-counter-\nbalanced. sanic is ahead at 32 and 14.6% behind at 1024; the crossover is\nbetween 32 and 128. Weight streaming is flat across that range, so all 4.8 ms\nof GPU regression is attention over the KV cache — against a working set of\n33.75 MB, which is 0.19 ms of streaming.\n\nThree independent causes, separately fixable and separately verifiable:\n\n  A  the output fold walks V with a 128-byte stride, so every load fetches a\n     cache line and uses 2 bytes of it — 3.4 GB/s, 53x off roofline, +4.7 ms\n  B  update_cache is functional, so the emitted map rewrites all 1030 cache\n     positions every step to change one — 67.5 MB of traffic for 32 KB, +1.06 ms\n  C  the scores fold is contiguous but latency-bound: 1030 threadgroups of\n     128 bytes is nowhere near enough in flight — 8.0 GB/s, +1.9 ms\n\nRecords what a fix is worth (A+B alone puts us ahead of mlx-lm at 1024), the\ntraps that would eat a session — notably that the obvious fix for A reduces\nthread count, a shape already measured at 0.61x — and an acceptance test that\nis flatness in context length rather than the 1024 endpoint.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01A4LnMq5EEzyfqvGMh7VFBL\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a4f6149f6939f7a2363b1d53270d53c555d38c73","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/trees/a4f6149f6939f7a2363b1d53270d53c555d38c73"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/commits/edfc5e1c5ff846ab473882b51174cc61a58e3488","verification":{"payload":"tree a4f6149f6939f7a2363b1d53270d53c555d38c73\nparent 95ed7c5f9fd132ed4a053ebca720c0827ceba684\nauthor Léo <44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com> 1785256276 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785256276 -0700\n\nattention doesn''t scale with context: the whole 1024-token gap, in three defects (#15)\n\nMeasured sanic vs mlx-lm at 32/128/512/1024 generated tokens, ABBA-counter-\nbalanced. sanic is ahead at 32 and 14.6% behind at 1024; the crossover is\nbetween 32 and 128. Weight streaming is flat across that range, so all 4.8 ms\nof GPU regression is attention over the KV cache — against a working set of\n33.75 MB, which is 0.19 ms of streaming.\n\nThree independent causes, separately fixable and separately verifiable:\n\n  A  the output fold walks V with a 128-byte stride, so every load fetches a\n     cache line and uses 2 bytes of it — 3.4 GB/s, 53x off roofline, +4.7 ms\n  B  update_cache is functional, so the emitted map rewrites all 1030 cache\n     positions every step to change one — 67.5 MB of traffic for 32 KB, +1.06 ms\n  C  the scores fold is contiguous but latency-bound: 1030 threadgroups of\n     128 bytes is nowhere near enough in flight — 8.0 GB/s, +1.9 ms\n\nRecords what a fix is worth (A+B alone puts us ahead of mlx-lm at 1024), the\ntraps that would eat a session — notably that the obvious fix for A reduces\nthread count, a shape already measured at 0.61x — and an acceptance test that\nis flatness in context length rather than the 1024 endpoint.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01A4LnMq5EEzyfqvGMh7VFBL\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqaNlUCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA01oQAA3Ju1n4W6wm+0U3Z+cMBHLh\nOGM8xiuzePC0KADKMjahmCdw8AgfUByJG9Q4bonZOQFTXFecBnhBJo/CW/MzXCp9\nBy9P6IPdfLXudj6ZqhxYTlTgJNVAU87UhdD/ttCM2cchHIHXYu0B+6wUuGjYZpWj\n/+JrvnTiNKjhpTVjsv1y27ZAN3baTgCxCBipefVAd4OXWr0VMaTKo0f4u0s2/rrR\niWVPsHn7M476d/Oj5n5Ooh4J2IXEVsZgEpGtgxf9AyHRIEbEgwBPCZ0LEOeVSQC5\n9tWnBx0bIdfaBeBTCp6GiyTDkdz+noBb/hBwp7HNCmIsHQJl1aFJpPIJ7v8qUhu1\nBA13mnlMZLWDhLCwiBN0QtvWXex1Lv4W1feq8iOr/hAo30Q/WfXFN4zCnLWlM1im\njni1eOP7wR7DtGHMFsBTG686zUKEiaqmnpbh5x16A1vePmzx2SF903gFscgZLhsx\nQRIKnlzGxtMBtSMOTZDQRtwvyLwq0/gV3tA+mRq1Hl8XcBC574JHTUKmzw3RHnDm\nPnv9/Fe7n2yoaaAtX5oDDjIAilm9H11e4xoEWl3ft5vA0BxCkwswUgJvSPFvvDam\nBmyAixPf5Hc2pZ7E5dLWdLTf6kwVp5DMfA3ZK1CncZihP51ABp0NWoYsdgJ+ZZm5\no2Rrp791JetbvjJFl2dd\n=3dpO\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-28T16:31:17Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/edfc5e1c5ff846ab473882b51174cc61a58e3488","node_id":"C_kwDOTBf03doAKGVkZmM1ZTFjNWZmODQ2YWI0NzM4ODJiNTExNzRjYzYxYTU4ZTM0ODg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe/sanic/commit/95ed7c5f9fd132ed4a053ebca720c0827ceba684","sha":"95ed7c5f9fd132ed4a053ebca720c0827ceba684","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/95ed7c5f9fd132ed4a053ebca720c0827ceba684"}],"sha":"edfc5e1c5ff846ab473882b51174cc61a58e3488","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/edfc5e1c5ff846ab473882b51174cc61a58e3488"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/44068562?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Leikoe","id":44068562,"login":"Leikoe","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0MDY4NTYy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Leikoe","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/commits/95ed7c5f9fd132ed4a053ebca720c0827ceba684/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-28T05:33:04Z","email":"44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Léo"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-28T05:33:04Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"applegpu/: measured facts about the Apple GPU stack (#14)\n\n* applegpu/: measured facts about the Apple GPU stack, and the probes behind them\n\nSix documents, ~550 lines, no vendor claim repeated without a measurement\nbeside it. Every number names the probe or the binary it came from, and\ninferences are labelled as inferences.\n\n  hardware.md   what the machine reports about itself — LPDDR5/Hynix/16 GB,\n                128 B line, 14 GPU cores, and DVFS ladders decoded from the\n                device tree including the memory controller''s 300-1500 MHz,\n                which I have not found published. Also what is NOT exposed:\n                bus width and channel count appear nowhere, so 204.8 GB/s\n                theoretical is a marketing figure, not a machine-readable fact.\n  bandwidth.md  the rated number is fabric capacity, not per-client: one GPU\n                client saturates at 179.7 cold / 188 best, GPU+CPU reach 210.5.\n                Scaling is linear to 14 threadgroups then flat, which with a\n                measured 219 ns latency puts the cap at ~23 outstanding cache\n                lines per core. Traversal locality is worth 48%; bytes per\n                dispatch follow t(S) = S/179.7 GB/s + 4.2 us, which predicts a\n                llama step''s per-class rates without knowing the kernels.\n  counters.md   the 31 GPU counters are gated in-process but readable out of\n                process, with a working xctrace recipe and the id/ref XML\n                parsing scheme. Measured at peak read: Buffer Read Limiter 96%,\n                MMU Limiter 84%, ALU 6%.\n  execution.md  encoder boundaries cost 10-16us, barriers 0.3; command buffers\n                on one queue execute in commit order; ICBs unstable past ~64\n                commands on Apple7; load pins the GPU at P6 1296 MHz and the\n                state cannot be pinned, only measured.\n  msl.md        default fast math deletes -inf masks; `half` is reserved; load\n                WIDTH does not matter but grid width does; a fusion that\n                reduces thread count is a regression at 0.61x.\n\nCarries the probes that produced them (bandwidth, wide-loads, residual-stream),\nthe runtime surface they needed (private allocation, texture views over\nbuffers, parallel-queue replay), and a pre-commit hook running fmt and clippy\nfor host AND x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu — the linux leg is the one that breaks,\nbecause everything Metal is cfg''d out there and macOS-only code turns into\ndead-code errors a host clippy cannot see. Two such breaks are fixed here.\n\nAlso records the two ways this work repeatedly fooled itself: measurement\nregimes that do not transfer, and always-baseline-first A/B ordering.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* inline_pays returns false 93 times out of 93: the recompute term is wrong\n\nChasing \"why does q not fuse its norm when k and v do\" found something larger.\ninline_pays decides every leaf cut in the partitioner, and on a llama step it\nnever once says inline.\n\nsubtree_read_bytes walks input_axes over the RAW node, so it sums every input\nreachable beneath it — for anything deep in the model, that is every prior\nlayer''s weights:\n\n  out_vol=8192 vol=2048 replicas=256 recompute=90996us round_trip=0.16us\n  bytes=2249406472\n\n2.2 GB of \"recompute\" for a node of volume 2048, 91 ms against a 0.16 us round\ntrip. No cone clears that bar, so the inequality has degenerated to \"always\nmaterialize\" and the cost model is not ranking anything.\n\nThe fix is the one item 7a already learned: price the SPLICED view, which stops\nat producers already materialized, rather than the raw subtree. What a consumer\nrebuilds is the cone down to its buffer reads, not the model''s whole history.\n\nThe visible symptom: k/v absorb their norm algebraically via defer-div while q\ngets a materialized one — two kernels per layer, 32 total, whose only consumer\nis the q projection. The post-attention norm is the honest case, since gate and\nup both read it. Nothing distinguishes the two today because the pricing cannot\ndiscriminate at all. Worth ~0.18 ms/step, and a precondition for trusting any\nother fusion decision.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* price recompute on what a consumer rebuilds, not on the model''s whole history\n\nsubtree_read_bytes walked input_axes over the RAW node, summing every input\nreachable beneath it — for anything deep in a transformer, every prior layer''s\nweight:\n\n  before: out_vol=8192 vol=2048 bytes=2249406472 recompute=90996us rt=0.164us\n  after:  out_vol=8192 vol=2048 bytes=8192       recompute=0.328us rt=0.164us\n\n2.2 GB and 91 ms of \"recompute\" for a cone of 2048 elements. rebuild_cost walks\ndown to what a consumer would actually rebuild and stops where the value will be\na buffer read: at a producer already in `done`, and at any fold-bearing node,\nsince leaf_cuts cuts those unconditionally and pricing happens on the way DOWN,\nbefore anything below has been emitted. k/v go 117 MB -> 12 KB, lm_head 16.8 MB\n-> 8 KB.\n\nThe verdicts do not change and llama''s schedule is identical — 278 kernels, same\ntext, 225 tests, bf16 and f32 unchanged. What changes is that the model now\ndecides for reasons that survive inspection: recomputing a 2048-element cone 256\ntimes prices at 84us against 44us to materialize, a real comparison, where\nbefore every cone lost to a number no cone could beat.\n\nCorrects the claim that motivated this. I reported inline_pays answering false\n93/93 and called it \"ranking nothing\"; that count came from instrumentation\nfiltered to volume >= 2048. The full distribution is 93 false, 128 true — small\ncones were being inlined all along. The pricing was wrong in magnitude, not\ndegenerate.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* issue writeup: the cost model has no cache term\n\nStandalone handoff for a fresh session — assumes no context.\n\nThe model prices every byte at DRAM rates, so re-reading a 4 KB activation 2048\ntimes is charged as 8 MB of DRAM traffic when it is one DRAM read and 2047 cache\nhits. Every fusion decision that recomputes a small hot operand is mispriced\ntoward materializing it.\n\nThe evidence is a disagreement between algebra and pricing that measurement\nsettles: k/v projections fuse their input norm via defer-div, so 512\nthreadgroups each re-read the activation, and the class still sustains 120 GB/s\non 2 MB — exactly what the size law predicts for the weight ALONE. The re-reads\nare free because 4 KB stays in cache. inline_pays prices the same cone through a\nDRAM rate and says materialize.\n\nThe writeup carries the acceptance test that makes it falsifiable: the input\nnorm feeding q (one consumer) must fuse, while the post-attention norm feeding\ngate AND up (two consumers) must stay materialized. A change that makes both go\nthe same way has not solved it.\n\nAlso carries the measured constants a fix needs — 179.7 GB/s DRAM, 219 ns\nlatency, 4.2 us per dispatch, ~24 MB SLC, and cache bandwidth >=332 GB/s, which\nis the closest thing to a measured constant for the new term — the verification\nsteps, and the four measurement traps this repo has already paid for.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c2b33f749bf2a57db5237f47be40ad57d2db0e0c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/trees/c2b33f749bf2a57db5237f47be40ad57d2db0e0c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Leikoe/sanic/git/commits/95ed7c5f9fd132ed4a053ebca720c0827ceba684","verification":{"payload":"tree c2b33f749bf2a57db5237f47be40ad57d2db0e0c\nparent f9e11e21fa422ec0f2c27f9ebe8e26340186df93\nauthor Léo <44068562+Leikoe@users.noreply.github.com> 1785216784 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785216784 -0700\n\napplegpu/: measured facts about the Apple GPU stack (#14)\n\n* applegpu/: measured facts about the Apple GPU stack, and the probes behind them\n\nSix documents, ~550 lines, no vendor claim repeated without a measurement\nbeside it. Every number names the probe or the binary it came from, and\ninferences are labelled as inferences.\n\n  hardware.md   what the machine reports about itself — LPDDR5/Hynix/16 GB,\n                128 B line, 14 GPU cores, and DVFS ladders decoded from the\n                device tree including the memory controller''s 300-1500 MHz,\n                which I have not found published. Also what is NOT exposed:\n                bus width and channel count appear nowhere, so 204.8 GB/s\n                theoretical is a marketing figure, not a machine-readable fact.\n  bandwidth.md  the rated number is fabric capacity, not per-client: one GPU\n                client saturates at 179.7 cold / 188 best, GPU+CPU reach 210.5.\n                Scaling is linear to 14 threadgroups then flat, which with a\n                measured 219 ns latency puts the cap at ~23 outstanding cache\n                lines per core. Traversal locality is worth 48%; bytes per\n                dispatch follow t(S) = S/179.7 GB/s + 4.2 us, which predicts a\n                llama step''s per-class rates without knowing the kernels.\n  counters.md   the 31 GPU counters are gated in-process but readable out of\n                process, with a working xctrace recipe and the id/ref XML\n                parsing scheme. Measured at peak read: Buffer Read Limiter 96%,\n                MMU Limiter 84%, ALU 6%.\n  execution.md  encoder boundaries cost 10-16us, barriers 0.3; command buffers\n                on one queue execute in commit order; ICBs unstable past ~64\n                commands on Apple7; load pins the GPU at P6 1296 MHz and the\n                state cannot be pinned, only measured.\n  msl.md        default fast math deletes -inf masks; `half` is reserved; load\n                WIDTH does not matter but grid width does; a fusion that\n                reduces thread count is a regression at 0.61x.\n\nCarries the probes that produced them (bandwidth, wide-loads, residual-stream),\nthe runtime surface they needed (private allocation, texture views over\nbuffers, parallel-queue replay), and a pre-commit hook running fmt and clippy\nfor host AND x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu — the linux leg is the one that breaks,\nbecause everything Metal is cfg''d out there and macOS-only code turns into\ndead-code errors a host clippy cannot see. Two such breaks are fixed here.\n\nAlso records the two ways this work repeatedly fooled itself: measurement\nregimes that do not transfer, and always-baseline-first A/B ordering.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* inline_pays returns false 93 times out of 93: the recompute term is wrong\n\nChasing \"why does q not fuse its norm when k and v do\" found something larger.\ninline_pays decides every leaf cut in the partitioner, and on a llama step it\nnever once says inline.\n\nsubtree_read_bytes walks input_axes over the RAW node, so it sums every input\nreachable beneath it — for anything deep in the model, that is every prior\nlayer''s weights:\n\n  out_vol=8192 vol=2048 replicas=256 recompute=90996us round_trip=0.16us\n  bytes=2249406472\n\n2.2 GB of \"recompute\" for a node of volume 2048, 91 ms against a 0.16 us round\ntrip. No cone clears that bar, so the inequality has degenerated to \"always\nmaterialize\" and the cost model is not ranking anything.\n\nThe fix is the one item 7a already learned: price the SPLICED view, which stops\nat producers already materialized, rather than the raw subtree. What a consumer\nrebuilds is the cone down to its buffer reads, not the model''s whole history.\n\nThe visible symptom: k/v absorb their norm algebraically via defer-div while q\ngets a materialized one — two kernels per layer, 32 total, whose only consumer\nis the q projection. The post-attention norm is the honest case, since gate and\nup both read it. Nothing distinguishes the two today because the pricing cannot\ndiscriminate at all. Worth ~0.18 ms/step, and a precondition for trusting any\nother fusion decision.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* price recompute on what a consumer rebuilds, not on the model''s whole history\n\nsubtree_read_bytes walked input_axes over the RAW node, summing every input\nreachable beneath it — for anything deep in a transformer, every prior layer''s\nweight:\n\n  before: out_vol=8192 vol=2048 bytes=2249406472 recompute=90996us rt=0.164us\n  after:  out_vol=8192 vol=2048 bytes=8192       recompute=0.328us rt=0.164us\n\n2.2 GB and 91 ms of \"recompute\" for a cone of 2048 elements. rebuild_cost walks\ndown to what a consumer would actually rebuild and stops where the value will be\na buffer read: at a producer already in `done`, and at any fold-bearing node,\nsince leaf_cuts cuts those unconditionally and pricing happens on the way DOWN,\nbefore anything below has been emitted. k/v go 117 MB -> 12 KB, lm_head 16.8 MB\n-> 8 KB.\n\nThe verdicts do not change and llama''s schedule is identical — 278 kernels, same\ntext, 225 tests, bf16 and f32 unchanged. What changes is that the model now\ndecides for reasons that survive inspection: recomputing a 2048-element cone 256\ntimes prices at 84us against 44us to materialize, a real comparison, where\nbefore every cone lost to a number no cone could beat.\n\nCorrects the claim that motivated this. I reported inline_pays answering false\n93/93 and called it \"ranking nothing\"; that count came from instrumentation\nfiltered to volume >= 2048. The full distribution is 93 false, 128 true — small\ncones were being inlined all along. The pricing was wrong in magnitude, not\ndegenerate.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* issue writeup: the cost model has no cache term\n\nStandalone handoff for a fresh session — assumes no context.\n\nThe model prices every byte at DRAM rates, so re-reading a 4 KB activation 2048\ntimes is charged as 8 MB of DRAM traffic when it is one DRAM read and 2047 cache\nhits. Every fusion decision that recomputes a small hot operand is mispriced\ntoward materializing it.\n\nThe evidence is a disagreement between algebra and pricing that measurement\nsettles: k/v projections fuse their input norm via defer-div, so 512\nthreadgroups each re-read the activation, and the class still sustains 120 GB/s\non 2 MB — exactly what the size law predicts for the weight ALONE. The re-reads\nare free because 4 KB stays in cache. inline_pays prices the same cone through a\nDRAM rate and says materialize.\n\nThe writeup carries the acceptance test that makes it falsifiable: the input\nnorm feeding q (one consumer) must fuse, while the post-attention norm feeding\ngate AND up (two consumers) must stay materialized. A change that makes both go\nthe same way has not solved it.\n\nAlso carries the measured constants a fix needs — 179.7 GB/s DRAM, 219 ns\nlatency, 4.2 us per dispatch, ~24 MB SLC, and cache bandwidth >=332 GB/s, which\nis the closest thing to a measured constant for the new term — the verification\nsteps, and the four measurement traps this repo has already paid for.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqaD8QCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAARMwQAJTr3UI4RGibkAM/jNJxv+Kw\nBfnaka6ehWSiQyt6xQV5MFsLNP8theyCbwrSpDOcmw5rMsBzT5desOVtidqvnDll\ntOW963BxX057+iibvIssfBwgMkjmZLpy/oNdfHduBGGgiv51fNWkN9Z0MVj0wBeu\ni0IBQv7CUfNZKLZU0R+hUplXxTqfknNQOnuM0RAGLjaoNgk+VFr4pLcZOWOrj7Vi\nCtH++UPngUfdgkAZRbpNWelJelG8Re6aORW4iW4+tO2yoZCethLtATEvFwyTIhTp\nwMxgwvU+ShI3Vc+zfGYCIE6+G+o7l0Ta2p0GlbWjB4fpc25nBHUzw5cpRfQhVCte\n9PSid7kUnDL3n5oj4tuHbl+7Iusz2jsEW13qm8aZ0sYVWSkceuoIFNCQrBZ9g7L9\nyaA62429rWN8t4+NSOwCmzKQ7slwx9YjVJOU52zdxAud4dkE1W87GThTOYspnZOM\n8rY54hf9fr8FMZYWKNnZqiev41x702JGFPbNu63zh18JaucWOd1LKYRZ9hMw+Ku6\nBMC5fSunBYch3xck6F7M+0zQnCt5ToWozH5qQJFGzPzoLIQcKIK6tVavLdxOT01o\nldlk68oLAsfdUtGlD3I4CTdIYzxsNQLkvW8h2FBc2CZr7XdsAyy1t2IHen7SZ2tS\nCVuyn77ySyO0z5peturI\n=K6FV\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-28T05:33:05Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","orga